window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2022-12-1", "source": "2022年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: In this task, you are to write an essay on the role of physical exercise in achieving success at college. You will have 30 minutes for the task. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "While many college students bury themselves in books and treat workouts as a waste of time, it is often\noverlooked that physical exercise plays an indispensable role in achieving success at college. In my view,\nstaying active is not the enemy of study but its solid foundation.\nFirst, regular exercise strengthens the body and guarantees the energy that intense coursework demands. A\nstudent who jogs every morning is more likely to stay focused through back-to-back lectures than one who\nnever moves. Moreover, working out is a powerful way to relieve stress. When exams and job hunting pile up,\na quick run on the track can wash away tension and rebuild mental resilience. In addition, sticking to a fixed\nexercise routine cultivates self-discipline, and this habit naturally transfers to managing study plans on time.\nIn short, physical exercise is by no means a distraction; rather, it is the cornerstone of college success.\nTherefore, every student is advised to fit regular workouts into daily life before body and mind break down."}, "translation": {"prompt": "在中国农历中，立秋 (Start of Autumn)意味着夏天的结束和秋天的开始。立秋带来的首先是天气的变化，气温逐渐下降。人们看到树叶开始变黄飘落时，知道秋天已经来临，这就是所谓的\"一叶知秋\"。但此时酷热的天气并未完全结束，高温通常还会持续一段时间，被称为\"秋老虎\"。立秋对农民意义重大，这时各种秋季作物迅速生长、开始成熟，收获的季节即将到来。", "sample": "In the Chinese lunar calendar, the Start of Autumn marks the end of summer and the beginning of autumn. It first brings a change in the weather, as temperatures gradually drop. When people see the leaves begin to turn yellow and fall, they know that autumn has arrived; this is the so-called principle that \"one falling leaf heralds the coming of autumn.\" However, the hot weather has not completely ended at this time; high temperatures usually continue for a while, a period known as the \"Autumn Tiger.\" The Start of Autumn is of great significance to farmers, as various autumn crops grow rapidly and begin to ripen at this time, and the harvest season is approaching."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2022-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Researchers, writing in the journal Heart, pooled data from 23 studies and found that social isolation or feelings of loneliness were tied to an increased risk of coronary heart disease(冠心病) and strokes.\nThe studies included data from 181,006 men and women ages 18 and over. There were 4,628 coronary events and 3,002 strokes in follow-up periods  __26__  from three to 21 years. Three of the papers  __27__  loneliness, 18 looked at social isolation and two included both. Social isolation and loneliness were determined with questionnaires; the researchers depended on medical records and death  __28__  for determining coronary events and strokes.\nThe scientists found that loneliness and social isolation increased the  __29__  risk of having a heart attack or a death from heart disease by 29 percent, and the risk of stroke by 32 percent. There were no  __30__  between men and women.\n\"People have tended to focus from a policy point of view on  __31__  lonely people to make them more  __32__  ,\" said the lead author, Nicole K. Valtorta, a research fellow at the University of York in England. \"Our study  __33__  that if this is a risk factor, then we should be trying to prevent the risk factor in the first place.\"\nThe authors  __34__  that this was a review of observational studies and did not  __35__  cause and effect.", "matchIntro": "The hidden costs colleges don't want you to know about", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "This fall, thousands of college students from across the country will begin their undergraduate studies at colleges around the nation. They will inevitably pack too much to fit in their tiny dorm rooms. They will also carry with them a huge student loan debt, in addition to countless \"hidden\" out-of-pocket costs paid for by their bank accounts and the bank accounts of their families."}, {"label": "B", "text": "At my well-respected, private, four-year university in Washington, D.C., which boasts a yearly tuition of $44,046 not including room and board, I receive over $57,000 yearly in financial aid. As a student from a family that is struggling to make ends meet, my financial aid package is a combination of federal grants and federal work study, university merit scholarships and financial aid awards, and about $8,000 yearly in federally subsidized(有补贴的) and unsubsidized loans. On paper, my expenses and my financial aid just about even out. Off paper, they don't."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Universities today are in the business of making money, and mine is no exception. They hit me right out of the gate with a $160 fee to attend my freshman orientation, a price which does not include the cost of travel to and from the District. Almost every class has an associated fee not included in the cost of tuition, most between $40 and $100. Fees for lab science classes are the highest, and all students at my university are required to take at least one lab before they graduate. Buying a laptop proved a necessity and, thankfully, a relative bought me one as a gift. Renting a mini-fridge for my dorm room costs my roommates and me about $140 a year."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Schools will charge you whatever they can. The costs of any damage to the dorm, including elevators, bathrooms, and common areas, are billed to every person on a dorm floor, or even the entire building if they do not know who caused the damage. After I fell out of my bed twice during my freshman year, the university installed a railing—for $20, billed to my student account. My financial aid did not anticipate any of these costs, and so it did not cover them."}, {"label": "E", "text": "An Internet search of \"hidden costs\" of college turns up a host of articles on parent-centered websites on the college application process. These articles are almost always geared towards upper- and middle-class families. For students already struggling to pay tuition, these costs may be the least of their worries."}, {"label": "F", "text": "So what hidden costs should low-income students really be paying attention to? My college experience offers a few examples."}, {"label": "G", "text": "If you are a low-income student who will be attending school out-of-state, make sure you know if you can use your state benefits, such as the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program, or SNAP. It wasn't until after I had accepted admission to an out-of-state school that I learned that I could not use my Ohio Medicaid on campus for anything other than emergency care. My benefits became invalid the second I moved out of Ohio. After my freshman year, I had to opt for the school's insurance plan, which costs around $2,000 a year. Even if your school offers a flat-rate fee for a doctor's appointment at the student health center (mine is $20 a visit), these fees often do not include extra fees for lab tests or prescription medications(药物)."}, {"label": "H", "text": "If you plan on paying off bills in your student account with a credit card, be aware of any additional costs. My school charges an additional fee for the use of a credit card to settle outstanding charges, which can add upwards of 3% of the balance to your bill."}, {"label": "I", "text": "There is another depressing reality hidden within even the largest financial aid packages: Colleges often offer the most generous packages during freshman year as a way to attract new students. My family was careful to ask about the chances of financial aid being taken away after my freshman year. We were assured that, except for low grades or a raise in family income, no money would be taken away. We did not know to ask—and the school did not readily point out—that even if tuition rises, my financial aid package will stay the same. So when my university voted to raise tuition costs 3% at the end of my freshman year, my financial aid package remained the same and I was suddenly responsible for an additional $1,200 for the next year. The university administration will likely vote to raise costs at least once more before my graduation."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Yes, I chose to attend an expensive university far from my hometown. Yes, there were cheaper options. But there are promising students from struggling families across the nation who should not rule out their dream schools entirely. All things considered, I am paying significantly less than the ticket price of my university, and having an educational experience in Washington, D.C., that I would not have had anywhere else. As a low-income student from a down-and-out Rust Belt community, these educational experiences have enormous potential to brighten my future—and my family's future."}, {"label": "K", "text": "The key is to make sure that students and their families understand that hidden costs exist, and that they may prove problematic. Fill out a more comprehensive checklist, and be wary of listed prices that seem too low. Understand just how complicated the financial aid process is."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Students and families must also understand their ability to self-advocate. They should not pay student bills or excess fees blindly. If something does not look right, ask about it. If it still doesn't look right, negotiate it. In cases where parents are working multiple jobs, are less knowledgeable about college bureaucracies, have limited English language skills, or are not contributing financially to their child's education, the burden of self-advocacy will fall on the student. I understand the difficulty, and the embarrassment. But it is necessary."}, {"label": "M", "text": "In the grand scheme of things, however, colleges also must come to understand that the hidden fees they ask for may prove unmanageable for the very kinds of low-income or first-generation students they are trying desperately to attract."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "To write his 2010 book, The 5-Factor World Diet, nutritionist Harley Pasternak traveled to the healthiest countries around the world to learn more about what made their meals extra nourishing.\nHe noted that Japanese people ate a wonderful variety of seaweeds, and that Chinese people tried to include at least five different colors in every meal. Pasternak also came away with some valuable observations about how different the North American way of life was, compared with many other countries.\nFor starters, Americans eat much bigger portions than people in other countries. \"We don't prioritize eating seasonally or locally, and we also add lots of salt, sugar and thickening agents to our foods,\" explained Pasternak. Contrast that to the healthy Mediterranean, Nordic and Okinawan diets listed in Pasternak's book. They all seem to stick to the ethos(特质) of regional, seasonal produce.\nFor example, a traditional Mediterranean diet includes fruits, vegetables, whole grains, nuts and olive oil as the main components of nutritional intake. Fish, chicken and red wine make moderate appearances, while red meat, salt and sugar are used much less often. The benefits of a traditional Mediterranean diet have been studied since the 1970s, and researchers have found that living that olive oil life can help people lose weight, lower their heart disease risk and reverse diabetes.\nMost other healthy eating cultures also make meals an event—say, multiple courses around the family table, or a glass or two of red wine at a long lunch—as opposed to hastily wolfing down handfuls of cereal above the kitchen sink and calling it dinner.\nEach of the healthy eating cultures has its own unique feature. But Pasternak did take note of one unifying factor in all of the healthy societies he observed. \"The only overlapping feature in most of these healthy countries is that they all walk way more than the average American,\" said Pasternak, \"So really, regardless of what you're eating, if someone's walking four miles more than you each day, they're going to be a lot thinner and live a lot longer than you.\"", "47": "To write his 2010 book, The 5-Factor World Diet, nutritionist Harley Pasternak traveled to the healthiest countries around the world to learn more about what made their meals extra nourishing.\nHe noted that Japanese people ate a wonderful variety of seaweeds, and that Chinese people tried to include at least five different colors in every meal. Pasternak also came away with some valuable observations about how different the North American way of life was, compared with many other countries.\nFor starters, Americans eat much bigger portions than people in other countries. \"We don't prioritize eating seasonally or locally, and we also add lots of salt, sugar and thickening agents to our foods,\" explained Pasternak. Contrast that to the healthy Mediterranean, Nordic and Okinawan diets listed in Pasternak's book. They all seem to stick to the ethos(特质) of regional, seasonal produce.\nFor example, a traditional Mediterranean diet includes fruits, vegetables, whole grains, nuts and olive oil as the main components of nutritional intake. Fish, chicken and red wine make moderate appearances, while red meat, salt and sugar are used much less often. The benefits of a traditional Mediterranean diet have been studied since the 1970s, and researchers have found that living that olive oil life can help people lose weight, lower their heart disease risk and reverse diabetes.\nMost other healthy eating cultures also make meals an event—say, multiple courses around the family table, or a glass or two of red wine at a long lunch—as opposed to hastily wolfing down handfuls of cereal above the kitchen sink and calling it dinner.\nEach of the healthy eating cultures has its own unique feature. But Pasternak did take note of one unifying factor in all of the healthy societies he observed. \"The only overlapping feature in most of these healthy countries is that they all walk way more than the average American,\" said Pasternak, \"So really, regardless of what you're eating, if someone's walking four miles more than you each day, they're going to be a lot thinner and live a lot longer than you.\"", "48": "To write his 2010 book, The 5-Factor World Diet, nutritionist Harley Pasternak traveled to the healthiest countries around the world to learn more about what made their meals extra nourishing.\nHe noted that Japanese people ate a wonderful variety of seaweeds, and that Chinese people tried to include at least five different colors in every meal. Pasternak also came away with some valuable observations about how different the North American way of life was, compared with many other countries.\nFor starters, Americans eat much bigger portions than people in other countries. \"We don't prioritize eating seasonally or locally, and we also add lots of salt, sugar and thickening agents to our foods,\" explained Pasternak. Contrast that to the healthy Mediterranean, Nordic and Okinawan diets listed in Pasternak's book. They all seem to stick to the ethos(特质) of regional, seasonal produce.\nFor example, a traditional Mediterranean diet includes fruits, vegetables, whole grains, nuts and olive oil as the main components of nutritional intake. Fish, chicken and red wine make moderate appearances, while red meat, salt and sugar are used much less often. The benefits of a traditional Mediterranean diet have been studied since the 1970s, and researchers have found that living that olive oil life can help people lose weight, lower their heart disease risk and reverse diabetes.\nMost other healthy eating cultures also make meals an event—say, multiple courses around the family table, or a glass or two of red wine at a long lunch—as opposed to hastily wolfing down handfuls of cereal above the kitchen sink and calling it dinner.\nEach of the healthy eating cultures has its own unique feature. But Pasternak did take note of one unifying factor in all of the healthy societies he observed. \"The only overlapping feature in most of these healthy countries is that they all walk way more than the average American,\" said Pasternak, \"So really, regardless of what you're eating, if someone's walking four miles more than you each day, they're going to be a lot thinner and live a lot longer than you.\"", "49": "To write his 2010 book, The 5-Factor World Diet, nutritionist Harley Pasternak traveled to the healthiest countries around the world to learn more about what made their meals extra nourishing.\nHe noted that Japanese people ate a wonderful variety of seaweeds, and that Chinese people tried to include at least five different colors in every meal. Pasternak also came away with some valuable observations about how different the North American way of life was, compared with many other countries.\nFor starters, Americans eat much bigger portions than people in other countries. \"We don't prioritize eating seasonally or locally, and we also add lots of salt, sugar and thickening agents to our foods,\" explained Pasternak. Contrast that to the healthy Mediterranean, Nordic and Okinawan diets listed in Pasternak's book. They all seem to stick to the ethos(特质) of regional, seasonal produce.\nFor example, a traditional Mediterranean diet includes fruits, vegetables, whole grains, nuts and olive oil as the main components of nutritional intake. Fish, chicken and red wine make moderate appearances, while red meat, salt and sugar are used much less often. The benefits of a traditional Mediterranean diet have been studied since the 1970s, and researchers have found that living that olive oil life can help people lose weight, lower their heart disease risk and reverse diabetes.\nMost other healthy eating cultures also make meals an event—say, multiple courses around the family table, or a glass or two of red wine at a long lunch—as opposed to hastily wolfing down handfuls of cereal above the kitchen sink and calling it dinner.\nEach of the healthy eating cultures has its own unique feature. But Pasternak did take note of one unifying factor in all of the healthy societies he observed. \"The only overlapping feature in most of these healthy countries is that they all walk way more than the average American,\" said Pasternak, \"So really, regardless of what you're eating, if someone's walking four miles more than you each day, they're going to be a lot thinner and live a lot longer than you.\"", "50": "To write his 2010 book, The 5-Factor World Diet, nutritionist Harley Pasternak traveled to the healthiest countries around the world to learn more about what made their meals extra nourishing.\nHe noted that Japanese people ate a wonderful variety of seaweeds, and that Chinese people tried to include at least five different colors in every meal. Pasternak also came away with some valuable observations about how different the North American way of life was, compared with many other countries.\nFor starters, Americans eat much bigger portions than people in other countries. \"We don't prioritize eating seasonally or locally, and we also add lots of salt, sugar and thickening agents to our foods,\" explained Pasternak. Contrast that to the healthy Mediterranean, Nordic and Okinawan diets listed in Pasternak's book. They all seem to stick to the ethos(特质) of regional, seasonal produce.\nFor example, a traditional Mediterranean diet includes fruits, vegetables, whole grains, nuts and olive oil as the main components of nutritional intake. Fish, chicken and red wine make moderate appearances, while red meat, salt and sugar are used much less often. The benefits of a traditional Mediterranean diet have been studied since the 1970s, and researchers have found that living that olive oil life can help people lose weight, lower their heart disease risk and reverse diabetes.\nMost other healthy eating cultures also make meals an event—say, multiple courses around the family table, or a glass or two of red wine at a long lunch—as opposed to hastily wolfing down handfuls of cereal above the kitchen sink and calling it dinner.\nEach of the healthy eating cultures has its own unique feature. But Pasternak did take note of one unifying factor in all of the healthy societies he observed. \"The only overlapping feature in most of these healthy countries is that they all walk way more than the average American,\" said Pasternak, \"So really, regardless of what you're eating, if someone's walking four miles more than you each day, they're going to be a lot thinner and live a lot longer than you.\"", "51": "Recognizing when a friend or colleague feels sad, angry or surprised is key to getting along with others. But a new study suggests that being sensitive to people's feelings may sometimes come with an extra dose of stress. This and other research challenge the prevailing view that emotional intelligence is uniformly beneficial to its bearer.\nIn a study, psychologist Myriam Bechtoldt of the Frankfurt School of Finance and Management in Germany asked 166 male university students a series of questions to measure their emotional intelligence. For example, they showed the students photographs of people's faces and asked them to what extent feelings such as happiness or disgust were being expressed. The students then had to give job talks in front of judges who displayed serious facial expressions. The scientists measured concentrations of stress hormones in the students' saliva(唾液) before and after the talk.\nIn students who were rated more emotionally intelligent, the stress measures increased more during the experiment and took longer to go back to baseline. The findings suggest that some people may be too emotionally clever for their own good, says Bechtoldt. \"Sometimes you can be so good at something that it causes trouble,\" she notes.\nIndeed, the study adds to previous research hinting at a dark side of emotional intelligence. A study published in 2002 in Personality and Individual Differences suggested that emotionally perceptive people might be particularly influenced by feelings of depression and hopelessness. Furthermore, several studies have implied that emotional intelligence can be used to manipulate others for personal gains.\nMore research is needed to see how exactly the relation between emotional intelligence and stress would play out in women and in people of different ages and education levels. Nevertheless, emotional intelligence is a useful skill to have, as long as you learn to also properly cope with emotions—both others' and your own, says Bechtoldt. For example, some sensitive individuals may assume responsibility for other people's sadness or anger, which ultimately stresses them out. Remember, as Bechtoldt says, \"you are not responsible for how other people feel.\"", "52": "Recognizing when a friend or colleague feels sad, angry or surprised is key to getting along with others. But a new study suggests that being sensitive to people's feelings may sometimes come with an extra dose of stress. This and other research challenge the prevailing view that emotional intelligence is uniformly beneficial to its bearer.\nIn a study, psychologist Myriam Bechtoldt of the Frankfurt School of Finance and Management in Germany asked 166 male university students a series of questions to measure their emotional intelligence. For example, they showed the students photographs of people's faces and asked them to what extent feelings such as happiness or disgust were being expressed. The students then had to give job talks in front of judges who displayed serious facial expressions. The scientists measured concentrations of stress hormones in the students' saliva(唾液) before and after the talk.\nIn students who were rated more emotionally intelligent, the stress measures increased more during the experiment and took longer to go back to baseline. The findings suggest that some people may be too emotionally clever for their own good, says Bechtoldt. \"Sometimes you can be so good at something that it causes trouble,\" she notes.\nIndeed, the study adds to previous research hinting at a dark side of emotional intelligence. A study published in 2002 in Personality and Individual Differences suggested that emotionally perceptive people might be particularly influenced by feelings of depression and hopelessness. Furthermore, several studies have implied that emotional intelligence can be used to manipulate others for personal gains.\nMore research is needed to see how exactly the relation between emotional intelligence and stress would play out in women and in people of different ages and education levels. Nevertheless, emotional intelligence is a useful skill to have, as long as you learn to also properly cope with emotions—both others' and your own, says Bechtoldt. For example, some sensitive individuals may assume responsibility for other people's sadness or anger, which ultimately stresses them out. Remember, as Bechtoldt says, \"you are not responsible for how other people feel.\"", "53": "Recognizing when a friend or colleague feels sad, angry or surprised is key to getting along with others. But a new study suggests that being sensitive to people's feelings may sometimes come with an extra dose of stress. This and other research challenge the prevailing view that emotional intelligence is uniformly beneficial to its bearer.\nIn a study, psychologist Myriam Bechtoldt of the Frankfurt School of Finance and Management in Germany asked 166 male university students a series of questions to measure their emotional intelligence. For example, they showed the students photographs of people's faces and asked them to what extent feelings such as happiness or disgust were being expressed. The students then had to give job talks in front of judges who displayed serious facial expressions. The scientists measured concentrations of stress hormones in the students' saliva(唾液) before and after the talk.\nIn students who were rated more emotionally intelligent, the stress measures increased more during the experiment and took longer to go back to baseline. The findings suggest that some people may be too emotionally clever for their own good, says Bechtoldt. \"Sometimes you can be so good at something that it causes trouble,\" she notes.\nIndeed, the study adds to previous research hinting at a dark side of emotional intelligence. A study published in 2002 in Personality and Individual Differences suggested that emotionally perceptive people might be particularly influenced by feelings of depression and hopelessness. Furthermore, several studies have implied that emotional intelligence can be used to manipulate others for personal gains.\nMore research is needed to see how exactly the relation between emotional intelligence and stress would play out in women and in people of different ages and education levels. Nevertheless, emotional intelligence is a useful skill to have, as long as you learn to also properly cope with emotions—both others' and your own, says Bechtoldt. For example, some sensitive individuals may assume responsibility for other people's sadness or anger, which ultimately stresses them out. Remember, as Bechtoldt says, \"you are not responsible for how other people feel.\"", "54": "Recognizing when a friend or colleague feels sad, angry or surprised is key to getting along with others. But a new study suggests that being sensitive to people's feelings may sometimes come with an extra dose of stress. This and other research challenge the prevailing view that emotional intelligence is uniformly beneficial to its bearer.\nIn a study, psychologist Myriam Bechtoldt of the Frankfurt School of Finance and Management in Germany asked 166 male university students a series of questions to measure their emotional intelligence. For example, they showed the students photographs of people's faces and asked them to what extent feelings such as happiness or disgust were being expressed. The students then had to give job talks in front of judges who displayed serious facial expressions. The scientists measured concentrations of stress hormones in the students' saliva(唾液) before and after the talk.\nIn students who were rated more emotionally intelligent, the stress measures increased more during the experiment and took longer to go back to baseline. The findings suggest that some people may be too emotionally clever for their own good, says Bechtoldt. \"Sometimes you can be so good at something that it causes trouble,\" she notes.\nIndeed, the study adds to previous research hinting at a dark side of emotional intelligence. A study published in 2002 in Personality and Individual Differences suggested that emotionally perceptive people might be particularly influenced by feelings of depression and hopelessness. Furthermore, several studies have implied that emotional intelligence can be used to manipulate others for personal gains.\nMore research is needed to see how exactly the relation between emotional intelligence and stress would play out in women and in people of different ages and education levels. Nevertheless, emotional intelligence is a useful skill to have, as long as you learn to also properly cope with emotions—both others' and your own, says Bechtoldt. For example, some sensitive individuals may assume responsibility for other people's sadness or anger, which ultimately stresses them out. Remember, as Bechtoldt says, \"you are not responsible for how other people feel.\"", "55": "Recognizing when a friend or colleague feels sad, angry or surprised is key to getting along with others. But a new study suggests that being sensitive to people's feelings may sometimes come with an extra dose of stress. This and other research challenge the prevailing view that emotional intelligence is uniformly beneficial to its bearer.\nIn a study, psychologist Myriam Bechtoldt of the Frankfurt School of Finance and Management in Germany asked 166 male university students a series of questions to measure their emotional intelligence. For example, they showed the students photographs of people's faces and asked them to what extent feelings such as happiness or disgust were being expressed. The students then had to give job talks in front of judges who displayed serious facial expressions. The scientists measured concentrations of stress hormones in the students' saliva(唾液) before and after the talk.\nIn students who were rated more emotionally intelligent, the stress measures increased more during the experiment and took longer to go back to baseline. The findings suggest that some people may be too emotionally clever for their own good, says Bechtoldt. \"Sometimes you can be so good at something that it causes trouble,\" she notes.\nIndeed, the study adds to previous research hinting at a dark side of emotional intelligence. A study published in 2002 in Personality and Individual Differences suggested that emotionally perceptive people might be particularly influenced by feelings of depression and hopelessness. Furthermore, several studies have implied that emotional intelligence can be used to manipulate others for personal gains.\nMore research is needed to see how exactly the relation between emotional intelligence and stress would play out in women and in people of different ages and education levels. Nevertheless, emotional intelligence is a useful skill to have, as long as you learn to also properly cope with emotions—both others' and your own, says Bechtoldt. For example, some sensitive individuals may assume responsibility for other people's sadness or anger, which ultimately stresses them out. Remember, as Bechtoldt says, \"you are not responsible for how other people feel.\""}, "questions": [{"id": "2022-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What happened at one of Australia's largest gold mines?", "options": ["A) Part of its dam wall collapsed.", "B) It released a lot of harmful gases.", "C) It was destroyed by an earthquake.", "D) Some miners were trapped underground."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Part of its dam wall collapsed.\n【定位】Operations at one of Australia's largest gold mines had to be temporarily suspended on Friday\nafter a partial wall collapse at one of the mine's dams.\n澳大利亚一座最大的金矿之一不得不在周五暂停作业，原因是该矿其中一个尾矿坝的坝体发\n生了局部坍塌。\n【信号】新闻导语首句锁定核心事件。Operations at one of Australia's largest gold mines had\nto be temporarily suspended on Friday after a partial wall collapse at one of the\nmine's dams. 是整篇新闻的导语句，结构「结果（停产）+ after + 原因（坝体局部坍\n塌）」完整地把核心事件交代清楚——after 之后的 partial wall collapse 就是事件本身，\n对应第 1 题答案。\n【替换】a partial wall collapse at one of the mine's dams ↔ Part of its dam wall collapsed.\n名词化 ↔ 主谓化\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音第 4 段 materials which are harmful to the environment and human health 出现\n了 harmful 这个词，但说的是「坝里盛着的矿渣可能对环境与人体有害」，并非「这次事件\n释放了有害气体」。选项 B 把 harmful 这个原词盗过来，再加上原文从未提及的 gases，\n制造了「听到 harmful 就选」的诱惑。\n借用原文：materials which are harmful to the environment and human health\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音第 2 段 The wall collapse at the Cadia mine came just a few days after two\nearthquakes hit the area. 只说「坝塌发生在两次地震几天之后」，并未说「金矿被地震毁\n了」；第 6 段更明说 The company was unable to confirm whether the recent\nearthquakes had contributed to the dam's wall collapse——连地震是否与坝塌有关都\n未确认。选项 C 把 earthquakes 这个原词盗过来，硬生生升级成「被地震毁」，是典型的\n过度推断。\n借用原文：The wall collapse at the Cadia mine came just a few days after two earthquakes hit\nthe area.\nD) 未提及\n录音从头到尾只讲「坝体局部坍塌、暂停作业、调查中」，第 7 段反而明说 the break had\nposed no safety threat to workers——发言人特意说事故对工人没有安全威胁。选项 D\n描述的「矿工被困井下」完全是矿难常见画面，凭考生的「常识联想」来骗人，原文无任何\n依据。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What did the spokesperson say about the incident?", "options": ["A) It posed a safety threat to the miners.", "B) It caused damage too heavy to assess.", "C) It brought the mine's operations to a halt.", "D) It was followed by two more earthquakes."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It brought the mine's operations to a halt.\n【定位】A company spokesperson said the operations at the site had been halted while the\ninvestigation is ongoing and that the break had posed no safety threat to workers.\n公司发言人表示，调查期间该矿区已停止作业，并称这次坝体破裂并未对工人构成任何安全\n威胁。\n【信号】spokesperson said 引述信号词。第 7 段开头 A company spokesperson said 是最强的\n引述信号，直接告诉考生「下面这句就是发言人的原话」。句中包含两个并列宾语从句\n（halted while investigation + no safety threat），把「停产」和「对工人无安全威胁」\n一并说出，正是第 2 题的答案句。\n【替换】the operations at the site had been halted ↔ It brought the mine's operations to a halt.\n被动 ↔ 主动 + 动词名词化\nhad to be temporarily suspended on Friday ↔ It brought the mine's operations to a halt.\nsuspend = halt\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音第 7 段明确说 the break had posed no safety threat to workers——发言人特意强\n调「没有」构成安全威胁。选项 A 把 posed、safety threat 这几个高辨识度原词原样照\n搬，唯独把 no 去掉，含义彻底反转。这是听力里最经典的「否定词陷阱」。\n借用原文：the break had posed no safety threat to workers\nB) 过度推断\n录音第 6 段说 said it was conducting a thorough investigation——公司正在做全面调\n查；选项 B 却把「在调查」升级成「损失大到无法评估」 It caused damage too heavy to\nassess.，凭空加上了 too heavy to assess 这一程度判断，原文无任何依据。\n借用原文：said it was conducting a thorough investigation\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音第 2 段说 The wall collapse at the Cadia mine came just a few days after two\nearthquakes hit the area.——坝塌发生在两次地震「之后」（事件 ← 两次地震在前）；\n选项 D It was followed by two more earthquakes. 却说「这次事件之后又有两次地\n震」，时序刚好相反，更多了 more 这个原文没有的词。选项 D 把 two earthquakes 这个\n原词盗过来，把时序对调。\n借用原文：The wall collapse at the Cadia mine came just a few days after two earthquakes hit\nthe area.\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Two boys and four girls were born on Monday to the surprise of a young couple and doctors who\nhad expected five babies.\n周一,一对年轻夫妇和原本预期会有五个婴儿的医生们惊讶地迎来了两个男孩和四个女孩的诞生。\n2 This was the first record of six babies being born at the same time in that region. The doctors\nprepared from early in the morning to help deliver five tiny citizens. They were in the operating room\nwith five teams of doctors, one for each baby.\n这是该地区首次有六个婴儿同时出生的记录。医生们从清晨就开始准备协助接生五个小公民。他们在手术\n室里准备了五组医生团队,每个婴儿配备一组。\n3 The first five babies were delivered successfully, and all baby beds were occupied. And then all of a\nsudden, it turned out there was another waiting to come out.\n前五个婴儿成功分娩,所有婴儿床都被占用了。然后突然之间,发现还有另一个婴儿等待出生。\n4 The doctor said the babies were in healthy condition, but could not go home immediately. They\nneeded to stay in the hospital for two to three months for medical supervision.\n医生表示婴儿们健康状况良好,但不能立即回家。他们需要在医院接受两到三个月的医学监护。\n5 The mother who is in stable condition could return home a day later if all physical tests came back\nnormal. The babies' mother commented that they had already prepared room for five babies at home, so\nthey will have to rearrange things for their happy surprise. The new parents have yet to name the sixth\nbaby but are considering either Lily or Rose.\n状况稳定的母亲如果所有体检结果正常,一天后就可以回家。婴儿的母亲表示他们已经在家里为五个婴\n儿准备了房间,所以他们必须为这个惊喜重新安排。这对新父母尚未给第六个婴儿取名,但正在考虑Lily\n或Rose。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What did the hospital do for the delivery of the babies?", "options": ["A) It prepared beds for all the six new citizens.", "B) It assigned a team of doctors for each expected baby.", "C) It made ample preparations for various possibilities.", "D) It brought in the most advanced instruments."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It assigned a team of doctors for each expected baby.\n【定位】The doctors prepared from early in the morning to help deliver five tiny citizens. They were\nin the operating room with five teams of doctors, one for each baby.\n医生们从清晨就开始做准备，迎接五个小公民的降生。他们和五组医生一起守在产房，每个\n宝宝都配一组医生。\n【信号】动词 prepared 直接对接题干 do。题干问 do，录音里出现 The doctors prepared from\nearly in the morning——同一语义场的动词 prepared 就是答案信号；紧接着的 five\nteams of doctors, one for each baby 把\"准备\"具体化为\"按预计人数一对一配\"，两句合起\n来构成完整答案句。\n【替换】five teams of doctors, one for each baby ↔ a team of doctors for each expected baby\none for each baby ↔ a team for each baby\nfive teams of doctors ↔ a team of doctors for each expected baby\n总数→分配视角的转换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt prepared beds for all the six new citizens. 把\"六个新公民都准备了床\"安在医院头上，\n但录音 all baby beds were occupied. And then all of a sudden, it turned out there\nwas another waiting to come out 说前五张床被占满后突然来了第六个，恰恰证明医院只\n准备了五张床，第六个是意外。选项里的 six 是听到的原词，却用在错误事实上。\n借用原文：all baby beds were occupied. And then all of a sudden, it turned out there was another\nwaiting to come out\nC)\nIt made ample preparations for various possibilities. 听起来像\"做了充分应对各种可能\n性的准备\"，但录音里没有任何\"应对多种可能性\"的话——医院其实只按五个宝宝准备，第\n六个是意外，恰恰说明它并未为\"各种可能\"做准备。这是用空泛大词诱选的典型。\n借用原文：all of a sudden, it turned out there was another waiting to come out\nD)\nIt brought in the most advanced instruments. 说医院引进了最先进的仪器，可录音通篇\n都没提到任何 instrument 或 advanced 之类的词，纯属无中生有。最高级 the most 一旦\n录音里没有等值表述就不能选。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What did the doctor say about the newborns?", "options": ["A) They had to undergo 2-3 physical checkups.", "B) They were all of normal size except the sixth.", "C) They could go home together with their mother a day later.", "D) They needed to stay in the hospital for a couple of months."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They needed to stay in the hospital for a couple of months.\n【定位】The doctor said the babies were in healthy condition, but could not go home immediately.\nThey needed to stay in the hospital for two to three months for medical supervision.\n医生说宝宝们身体状况良好，但不能马上回家。他们需要在医院留观两到三个月以接受医疗\n监护。\n【信号】the doctor said 直接领出答案句。录音里出现的 The doctor said 完美对应题干中的 the\ndoctor say，紧接着的 but 转折引出真正落点：could not go home immediately. They\nneeded to stay in the hospital for two to three months——but 之后才是答案。\n【替换】needed to stay in the hospital for two to three months ↔\nThey needed to stay in the hospital for a couple of months.\ntwo to three months ↔ a couple of months\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nThey had to undergo 2-3 physical checkups. 把录音里听到的 two to three 借走，却安\n在 physical checkups（体检次数）上——可录音里 two to three 修饰的是 months（月\n数）。这是把同一数字偷换修饰对象的典型原词陷阱。\n借用原文：two to three months for medical supervision\nB)\nThey were all of normal size except the sixth. 说\"除第六个外都是正常大小\"，但录音里\n医生只说 the babies were in healthy condition（健康），并未提任何\"大小是否正常\"，\n更没说第六个例外。这是\"加细节诱选\"。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey could go home together with their mother a day later. 把\"一天后回家\"安在宝宝\n身上，但录音 The mother who is in stable condition could return home a day later 明\n确指出 a day later 是妈妈的安排；而宝宝那边是 could not go home immediately。选项\n盗用了 a day later 这个原词，把妈妈的待遇张冠到宝宝头上。\n借用原文：The mother who is in stable condition could return home a day later", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn from the news report about the Spanish island Palmador?", "options": ["A) It is owned by the local government.", "B) It has been turned into a public park.", "C) It has been bought by an American.", "D) It is a perfect tourist destination."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is a perfect tourist destination.\n【定位】A Spanish island called Palmador has been bought by a family from Europe for 18 million\neuros. The island is often described as heaven on earth by holidaymakers.\n一个名叫帕尔马多尔的西班牙岛屿已被一个来自欧洲的家庭以 1800 万欧元买下。这个岛常\n被度假者形容为「人间天堂」。\n【信号】新闻导语+总括赞美句。答案信号有两层：一是开头第一句作为新闻导语，交代核心事实「a\nfamily from Europe（欧洲家庭）买下了岛」；二是紧接的总括赞美 often described as\nheaven on earth by holidaymakers——heaven on earth 这种总括式形容词，正是与选\n项 a perfect tourist destination 对应的同义信号。\n【替换】often described as heaven on earth by holidaymakers ↔ a perfect tourist destination\n总括赞美·同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项说岛 owned by the local government，但原文 has been bought by a family from\nEurope 与第三段 it was offered to the local government but proved too expensive\nfor the local authorities 明确说「曾打算卖给地方政府，但地方政府嫌太贵买不起」——\n所以买家是欧洲家庭，不是政府，A 反了。\n借用原文：it was offered to the local government but proved too expensive for the local authorities\nB) 偷换概念\n原文只说「它位于一个 natural park 里面」（its natural beauty is protected as it lies\ninside a natural park），是说岛位于自然公园之内、受保护；并没有说岛本身「被变成了\n公共公园」。选项 turned into a public park 偷换了「位于公园内」和「自身变成公\n园」。\n借用原文：its natural beauty is protected as it lies inside a natural park\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文 has been bought by a family from Europe 明确说买家来自欧洲（Europe），选项\nbought by an American 把国籍换成了美国人——动作（bought）原音照搬，国籍偷换，\n是典型的「原词陷阱+张冠李戴」组合。\n借用原文：has been bought by a family from Europe", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What do holidaymakers come to Palmador to enjoy?", "options": ["A) Its seafood.", "B) Its unusual coastline.", "C) Its unspoiled beaches.", "D) Its architecture."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Its unspoiled beaches.\n【定位】For decades tourists have been flocking to Palmador, which is located off the southeast coast\nof Spain. They come to enjoy its unspoiled beaches and crystal clear waters.\n几十年来，游客们一直涌向帕尔马多尔，它位于西班牙东南海岸外。他们来这里是为了享受\n未受破坏的海滩和清澈见底的海水。\n【信号】原音 come to enjoy 直接点题。答案信号是原音里 They come to enjoy its unspoiled\nbeaches and crystal clear waters 出现的 come to enjoy——它与题干 come to…enjoy\n完全同构，紧跟其后的两个名词短语就是答案的两个候选。\n【替换】its unspoiled beaches and crystal clear waters ↔ Its unspoiled beaches\n原词原音直接复现\n【排除】A) 未提及\n全文未出现任何与 seafood 相关的词。这项是预读时就预警过的「海岛+seafood」刻板印\n象陷阱——主体考生容易凭「海岛大概有好吃的海鲜」这种常识脑补出答案，但原文没说就\n是没说。\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文提及 coast（located off the southeast coast of Spain）只是说岛「位于西班牙东南\n海岸外」，是定位用语，不是描述岛本身的海岸线独特。选项 Its unusual coastline 借了\ncoast 这个原词，并加了一个原文没有的形容词 unusual。\n借用原文：located off the southeast coast of Spain\nD) 未提及\n原文通篇没有提到岛上有什么建筑（无任何 building / architecture / house 的描述），与\n选项 Its architecture 完全无对应原文。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What do estate agents say about Palmador?", "options": ["A) It has an unmatched location.", "B) It is worth over 18 million euros.", "C) It has beautiful weather all year round.", "D) It is an ideal place to meet famous stars."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It has an unmatched location.\n【定位】It is said to have a remarkable location that few other private islands of this size can match,\naccording to estate agents.\n据房产中介称，这座岛拥有非凡的地理位置，少有同等大小的其他私人岛屿能与之匹敌。\n【信号】引述标志 according to + 最高级语义。答案信号有两层：①引述定位词 according to\nestate agents 与题干 estate agents say 完全对应——这是这道题答案句的硬信号；②引\n述句内含最高级语义 few other private islands of this size can match（少有同等大小的\n私人岛能比得过），与选项 unmatched location 的最高级语义一一对应。\n【替换】a remarkable location that few other private islands of this size can match ↔\nIt has an unmatched location\n最高级语义·同义浓缩\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n18 million euros 确实出现在原音里（bought by a family from Europe for 18 million\neuros），但它是新闻导语里的成交价格，不是房产中介对这座岛的评价。预读时就把 18\nmillion euros 标为数字陷阱预警——正是为这一题做准备。\n借用原文：has been bought by a family from Europe for 18 million euros\nC) 未提及\n全文没有出现 weather / climate / sunshine 等任何与天气有关的描述。选项 beautiful\nweather all year round 是基于「度假胜地通常天气好」做的常识脑补，并不来自原音。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 its seafront is public land and is a favorite destination for famous people 说海滨\n是「名人爱来的地方」，但这与选项 an ideal place to meet famous stars（一个见明星的\n理想场所）不同——前者讲名人来此度假，后者讲普通人来此能「遇见」明星。同时该句也\n不是 estate agents 说的，而是新闻自己的陈述，连发话人都对不上。\n借用原文：its seafront is public land and is a favorite destination for famous people", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What do we learn about the woman from the conversation?", "options": ["A) She has been attending some group classes.", "B) She has registered for two new gym classes.", "C) She became a member of the gym two months ago.", "D) She is entitled to a discount on all the gym exercises."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） She became a member of the gym two months ago.\n【定位】Yes, I signed up two months ago, but I haven't been to any of the group classes yet.\n是的，我两个月前就注册了，不过我还一节团体课都没上过。\n【信号】被问者应答 = 答案落点。前一句 Are you already a member? 是询问者抛出的问题，被问\n的女士紧接着用 Yes, I signed up two months ago 给出直接应答——对话题里，问句之后\n被问者的应答几乎就是答案句。\n【替换】I signed up two months ago ↔ She became a member of the gym two months ago\nsigned up = became a member\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 She has been attending some group classes. 借用了录音 any of the group\nclasses 这一原词，但女士说的是 I haven't been to any of the group classes yet(还没上\n过)——典型听到原词就想选的原词陷阱，方向恰好相反。\n借用原文：I haven't been to any of the group classes yet\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 She has registered for two new gym classes. 把录音里 two of our brand new\nclasses 拼到女士头上，但全文女士只在最后报名了一门 hot yoga，根本没有「报了两\n门」，且打电话之初仍只是咨询阶段——属偷换「咨询/报名」的概念。\n借用原文：two of our brand new classes, hot yoga, and advanced spinning\nD)\n选项 She is entitled to a discount on all the gym exercises. 里的 all 是范围词陷阱：原\n文明确 the discount doesn't apply to any of our regular classes，折扣只限两门新课、\n不及于所有项目——把「局部」扩成「全部」。\n借用原文：the discount doesn't apply to any of our regular classes", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What is the gym center doing right now?", "options": ["A) Considering the promotion of its regular classes.", "B) Taking measures to expand its exercise programs.", "C) Recruiting coaches for hot yoga and advanced spinning.", "D) Offering existing members a discount off two new classes."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Offering existing members a discount off two new classes.\n【定位】Currently we are offering existing members discounts off two of our brand new classes, hot\nyoga, and advanced spinning, but the discount doesn't apply to any of our regular classes\nI'm afraid.\n目前我们正给老会员提供两门全新课程的折扣——热瑜伽和高级动感单车，但是这个折扣不\n适用于任何常规课程，很抱歉。\n【信号】时间副词 Currently 直接领出现行举措。Currently we are offering existing members\ndiscounts off two of our brand new classes 用 Currently+we are offering 把「当前正\n在做什么」摆得明明白白，正是题干 right now 的同义信号。\n【替换】we are offering existing members discounts off two of our brand new classes ↔\nOffering existing members a discount off two new classes.\n几乎逐字对应的同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Considering the promotion of its regular classes. 借用了录音里 regular classes 的\n原词，但原文是 the discount doesn't apply to any of our regular classes——常规课程\n恰恰是「不打折」的对象，健身房并未推广常规课程。\n借用原文：the discount doesn't apply to any of our regular classes\nB) 过度推断\n选项 Taking measures to expand its exercise programs. 里的「扩展锻炼项目」在录音里\n没有任何句子支撑——录音只说推出了两门新课的折扣，并未提「扩张/扩展」。属于把\n「推新课折扣」过度推断为「扩张项目」。\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Recruiting coaches for hot yoga and advanced spinning. 把录音 hot yoga, and\nadvanced spinning 这两个原词照搬过来，但行为是「招教练」——录音里从未提到招\n聘，是听到课程名就想选的典型原词陷阱。\n借用原文：two of our brand new classes, hot yoga, and advanced spinning", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the man say is a pity for the woman?", "options": ["A) She missed the deadline for the 10-week course.", "B) She missed out on the gym's general discount.", "C) She didn't sign up for membership in time.", "D) She wasn't so much interested in hot yoga."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） She missed out on the gym's general discount.\n【定位】The same discount of 20% is being offered to everyone at the door on a \"first come, first\nserved\" basis for the first month. It's a shame you missed out on the general discount.\n首月对所有人在现场都按「先到先得」提供同样的 20% 折扣。可惜你错过了这个普通\n折扣。\n【信号】惋惜句标志 It's a shame…。It's a shame you missed out on the general discount 用\nIt's a shame 把男方的「惋惜」明明白白说出口，正与题干 a pity 一一对应；shame 之后\n跟的就是「错过了什么」。\n【替换】you missed out on the general discount ↔ She missed out on the gym's general discount.\n几乎原词复现的同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 She missed the deadline for the 10-week course. 借用录音里 the 10-week\ncourse on Thursday evenings 的原词，但 10-week course 是女士最后真正报上的课、\n不存在「错过截止日」；男方的惋惜对象是 general discount，不是 10-week course。\n借用原文：Can you sign me up for the 10-week course on Thursday evenings?\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 She didn't sign up for membership in time. 与录音 I signed up two months ago\n直接冲突：女士两个月前已经入会，根本不存在「没及时入会」的问题。\n借用原文：I signed up two months ago\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 She wasn't so much interested in hot yoga. 与录音 I am only really interested in\nhot yoga 完全相反——女士只对热瑜伽感兴趣，而非「不感兴趣」。属盗用 hot yoga 原\n词的反向陷阱。\n借用原文：I am only really interested in hot yoga", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "Why is the woman unwilling to pay in advance?", "options": ["A) She doesn't want to reveal her card details over the phone.", "B) She doesn't think it wise to pay before attending any class.", "C) She might have to cancel her registration any minute.", "D) She prefers to have the fee added to her monthly bill."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） She doesn't want to reveal her card details over the phone.\n【定位】No, I don't like giving my card details over the phone anyway.\n不用，反正我也不喜欢在电话里报我的卡号细节。\n【信号】表态句 No+理由句 I don't like… 紧贴在一起。No 是女士对 Would you prefer to pay in\nadvance? 的明确拒绝，紧跟其后的 I don't like giving my card details over the phone\n就是拒绝的原因——这种「先表态、后给因」的结构是对话原因题的标志答案句。\n【替换】I don't like giving my card details over the phone ↔\nShe doesn't want to reveal her card details over the phone.\ndon't like giving = doesn't want to reveal\n【排除】B) 未提及\n选项 She doesn't think it wise to pay before attending any class. 听起来合理，但录音\n里女士的原因仅围绕「电话里报卡号」，完全没有「上课前付不明智」这种判断——属凭常\n识替原文补理由。\nC) 未提及\n选项 She might have to cancel her registration any minute. 中「随时可能退课」在录音\n里没有任何句子支持——女士反而紧接着报了 10-week course，并未表现出要退的迹象。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 She prefers to have the fee added to her monthly bill. 借用 the fee of the class\nwill just be added to your monthly bill 的原词，但 monthly bill 是男方在女士拒绝预付\n之后才提出的替代方案，并不是女士拒绝预付的原因——属把「结果」当「原因」的原词陷\n阱。\n借用原文：the fee of the class will just be added to your monthly bill", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "Why are the speakers in China?", "options": ["A) To make investments.", "B) To sign a business contract.", "C) To research new markets.", "D) To open a new office."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） To research new markets.\n【定位】There are lots of great potential markets here in China. So I'm sure that the head office in\nLondon will be pleased once we get back and present our research.\n这里中国市场潜力很大。我相信我们回去把调研结果汇报给伦敦总部，他们一定会满意的。\n【信号】research 一词定位。答案落点不靠『目的信号词 to do』，而是靠对此行成果的总结句\npresent our research。当对话里一方在概括『我们这趟做了什么/带回什么』，那句往往就\n是目的题的答案句——做的事就是来此的目的。\n【替换】present our research ↔ To research new markets.\n名词化转动作\npotential markets here in China ↔ To research new markets.\n潜在=新\n【排除】A)\n录音从头到尾没有 invest/investment/money 之类的词，To make investments. 属于\n『未提及』型干扰，靠的是听众对『商务出差=投资』的常识联想。\nB) 未提及\n录音没出现 contract/sign/deal 等词，To sign a business contract. 是『未提及』型干\n扰，与 A 同属『商务=签合同』的常识误判。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音多次出现 the head office in London，To open a new office. 盗用了 office 这个原\n词，让人听到 office 就想选；但录音里 office 始终指『已经存在的伦敦总部』，没有任何\n『开设新办公室』的动作。\n借用原文：the head office in London", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "Where will the man stop over on his way to London?", "options": ["A) Dubai.", "B) Beijing.", "C) Amsterdam.", "D) Earl's Court."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Dubai.\n【定位】There's a flight arriving the following morning, with a two-hour stopover in Dubai, or a flight\narriving at 11:30 in the evening, with a five-hour stopover in Amsterdam.\n有一班次日早晨到达、在迪拜中转两小时的航班；还有一班晚上 11:30 到达、在阿姆斯特丹\n中转五小时的航班。\n【信号】两项并列与男方应答。答案不在女方那句『二选一』里，而在男方第 5 段的应答 Well,\nthat's obvious then, isn't it? 之后、女方第 6 段的确认 okay, so that's Dubai. ——这是对\n话题的标志性信号：被问者/被选方的应答才是落点。\n【替换】so that's Dubai ↔ Dubai.\n确认句直给\n【排除】B)\nflights from Beijing to London 里 Beijing 是出发城市，不是中转地。Beijing. 盗用了这个\n原词，制造『一听到 Beijing 就选』的陷阱。\n借用原文：flights from Beijing to London\nC)\na five-hour stopover in Amsterdam 中 Amsterdam 确实是中转，但属于男方放弃的『晚\n到+长候机』那条线——男方那句 Well, that's obvious then, isn't it? 暗示选 short\nstopover（两小时的 Dubai）。Amsterdam. 盗用 Amsterdam 原词，但选错了那条线。\n借用原文：a five-hour stopover in Amsterdam\nD)\nThe nearest metro station is Earl's Court. 中 Earl's Court 是伦敦酒店附近的地铁站，是\n男方到达伦敦后的活动地点，与『中转』毫无关系。Earl's Court. 盗用了一个完全异类的地\n名做干扰。\n借用原文：The nearest metro station is Earl's Court.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the man ask the woman to do?", "options": ["A) Rent a bike for him to get around the town.", "B) Reserve a meeting room in the head office.", "C) Help him prepare his presentation.", "D) Send him a map of the hotel area."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Send him a map of the hotel area.\n【定位】Can you find a map that shows where the hotel is and send it to me online?\n你能找一份显示酒店位置的地图，然后线上发给我吗？\n【信号】Can you 请求信号。Can you find a map that shows where the hotel is and send it to\nme online? 是男方说出口的请求句，Can you 是最经典的请求信号——本题答案就在这一\n句里。\n【替换】find a map that shows where the hotel is and send it to me online ↔\nSend him a map of the hotel area.\n详述变概括\nsend it to me online ↔ Send him a map of the hotel area.\n代词与人称转换\n【排除】A)\nI want to see if I can get one of those rental bicycles and ride to the office just for\nfun. 这是男方自己想做的事，主语是 I，不是请求女方。Rent a bike for him to get\naround the town. 盗用了 rental bicycles 这个原词，制造『听到 bike 就选』的陷阱。\n借用原文：I want to see if I can get one of those rental bicycles and ride to the office just for fun.\nB)\nDo you remember the capacity of their meeting room? 男方只是问女方『记不记得容\n量』，I'll check. 是女方主动表示要去查，没人请她去『预订（reserve）会议室』。\nReserve a meeting room in the head office. 盗用 meeting room 原词、把 capacity 偷\n换成 reserve。\n借用原文：Do you remember the capacity of their meeting room?\nC)\nFor the presentation, I'll bring my own laptop and hook it up to their projector.\npresentation 由男方自己准备（I'll bring…），没有请求女方帮忙。Help him prepare his\npresentation. 盗用 presentation 原词，制造『听到就选』。\n借用原文：For the presentation, I'll bring my own laptop and hook it up to their projector.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What did the woman remind the man to do at the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) Bring his projector.", "B) Keep all his receipts.", "C) Submit his claims form.", "D) Pay with his credit card."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Keep all his receipts.\n【定位】No problem. Just remember to keep all your receipts. You remember the trouble you had\nlast time.\n没问题。记得把所有发票留好。你记得上次遇到的麻烦吧。\n【信号】Just remember to 提醒信号。答案信号是 Just remember to keep all your receipts.\n中的 Just remember to——这是英语里最典型的『提醒』句式，直接对应题干 remind…\nto do。\n【替换】Just remember to keep all your receipts. ↔ Keep all his receipts.\n祈使变陈述\n【排除】A)\nhook it up to their projector 中 projector 指对方公司已有的，男方说 I'll hook it up\n——根本没有『自带投影仪』的意思，更不是女方的提醒。Bring his projector. 是张冠李\n戴 + 与原文相反双重错。\n借用原文：hook it up to their projector\nC)\nI'll submit my claims form afterwards. 是男方自己说『事后我会提交报销单』，主语是\nI，不是女方提醒。Submit his claims form. 盗用 claims form 这个原词制造陷阱，让人听\n到 submit/claims 就选。\n借用原文：I'll submit my claims form afterwards.\nD)\nI'll pay everything with my own card, right? 是男方自己交代会用自己的卡付款，并非女\n方提醒。Pay with his credit card. 盗用 card 原词。\n借用原文：I'll pay everything with my own card, right?", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What did the researchers of the new study try to determine?", "options": ["A) Whether a country's educational level is linked to women's rights.", "B) Whether women's rights are making good progress around the world.", "C) Whether a country's protection of women's rights is related to its public health.", "D) Whether women's rights are more often overlooked in less-developed countries."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Whether a country's protection of women's rights is related to its public health.\n【定位】Thus, they wanted to determine if there was a link between protection of women's rights and\npublic health.\n因此，他们想要确定女性权利的保护与公共健康之间是否存在某种联系。\n【信号】因果信号 Thus 引出研究目的句。Thus, they wanted to determine if there was a link\nbetween protection of women's rights and public health. 由 Thus 引出，是前文铺垫之\n后的结论性目的句；同时 wanted to determine 与题干 try to determine 是同义改写，双\n重信号锁定本句为答案句。\n【替换】if there was a link between protection of women's rights and public health ↔\nWhether a country's protection of women's rights is related to its public health.\nif↔Whether、a link↔is related to 的同义替换\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 Whether a country's educational level is linked to women's rights. 用了一个录音\n根本没提的 educational level；录音只谈 protection of women's rights and public\nhealth，根本没有把教育水平作为研究对象，属于未提及式干扰。\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 Whether women's rights are making good progress around the world. 借用了录\n音 many parts of the world have made good economic progress 里的 good progress\n与 the world 两个原词，但录音说的是经济进展不错而女性权利常被忽视，并不是在问女性\n权利本身有没有进展；属于盗用原词答非所问。\n借用原文：many parts of the world have made good economic progress, but women's rights are\noften overlooked\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Whether women's rights are more often overlooked in less-developed\ncountries. 盗用录音原词 often overlooked，但录音只说 women's rights are often\noverlooked，并没有把这种忽视限定到 less-developed countries；选项擅自加了一个原\n文没有的范围限定。\n借用原文：women's rights are often overlooked", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the passage say about countries lacking in medical resources?", "options": ["A) Their people still have better health if women's rights are respected.", "B) They must make efforts to increase women's access to health care.", "C) Their people tend to attach importance to women's rights.", "D) They need to invest more in hospital staff and facilities."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Their people still have better health if women's rights are respected.\n【定位】Furthermore, in countries where women's rights were most respected, but where access to\nhospitals and doctors was below average, health outcomes were still better than in countries\nrated as moderate or poor.\n此外，在那些最尊重女性权利但医院与医生资源低于平均水平的国家里，健康结果仍然要好\n于那些评级为中等或较差的国家。\n【信号】转折信号 but+still 锁定反差结论。in countries where women's rights were most\nrespected, but where access to hospitals and doctors was below average, health\noutcomes were still better 中的 but 与 still 是双重转折/让步信号，听到 but…still 几乎就\n等于答案句敲钟，篇章里这种条件下仍如何的句子是出题点的常态。\n【替换】access to hospitals and doctors was below average ↔\nTheir people still have better health if women's rights are respected.\n缺医疗资源↔lacking medical resources 同义概括\nhealth outcomes were still better ↔ still have better health\nhealth outcomes↔health、still better↔still better 的近乎逐词等值\nin countries where women's rights were most respected ↔ if women's rights are respected\nwhere 从句条件化↔if 条件句的等值改写\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 They must make efforts to increase women's access to health care. 借用录音里\naccess to hospitals and doctors 的 access 一词，但录音是在客观描述这些国家医疗资源\n不足下仍有好的健康结果，根本没有说他们必须做什么；选项把客观陈述偷换成主观对策，\n又强加了 women's access to health care 这种原文没有的提法。\n借用原文：access to hospitals and doctors was below average\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项 Their people tend to attach importance to women's rights. 把录音里关于这些国\n家本身尊重女性权利的客观描述，偷换为这些国家的人民倾向于重视女性权利；录音说的是\n国家层面的尊重程度，并没有谈人民的态度倾向。\n借用原文：in countries where women's rights were most respected\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 They need to invest more in hospital staff and facilities. 借用了录音 hospitals\nand doctors 里的 hospitals，但录音从未说这些国家需要投入更多在医院上；选项把缺资\n源仍健康好这一反差客观结论，偷换成需要加大投入的对策。\n借用原文：access to hospitals and doctors was below average", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What has often been questioned regarding women's rights?", "options": ["A) Their link with a country's public health.", "B) Their potential impact on social progress.", "C) Their value to a country's international image.", "D) Their positive effect on economic development."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Their positive effect on economic development.\n【定位】They note that the value of women's rights has often been questioned from an\neconomic standpoint.\n他们指出，女性权利的价值常常从经济角度受到质疑。\n【信号】题干同根词 questioned 直接对应。the value of women's rights has often been\nquestioned from an economic standpoint 几乎是题干 What has often been\nquestioned regarding women's rights? 的逐词同根回响：questioned 对 questioned、\nwomen's rights 对 women's rights，定位极其稳。\n【替换】the value of women's rights has often been questioned from an economic standpoint ↔\nTheir positive effect on economic development.\nfrom an economic standpoint↔on economic development 的角度替换\nensuring those rights would limit progress, but this study indicates the opposite ↔\npositive effect on economic development\n否定的反面=正面效应的等值推理\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Their link with a country's public health. 借用了录音前文 protection of women's\nrights and public health 的 public health 一词，但 18 题问的是被质疑的内容，而录音里\n女性权利与公共健康的关系是被研究证实的，并非被质疑的对象；属于盗用原词答非所问。\n借用原文：protection of women's rights and public health\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 Their potential impact on social progress. 借用了录音 ensuring those rights\nwould limit progress 里的 progress，但录音里的 progress 是与 economic 挂钩的，且\n选项加了一个 social 修饰；录音从未谈过对 social progress 的潜在影响，属于偷换概念。\n借用原文：Some have argued that ensuring those rights would limit progress\nC) 未提及\n选项 Their value to a country's international image. 谈的是对国家国际形象的价值，录\n音从头到尾都没谈过 international image 或与之相关的概念，属于未提及式干扰。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the passage say about hot sunshine?", "options": ["A) It creates a wonderful setting for dating.", "B) It may cause strange physical reactions.", "C) It turns parks into picnic sites.", "D) It may result in a crowded beach."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It may cause strange physical reactions.\n【定位】Sunshine and high temperatures can mean picnics and parks and trips to beaches, but they can\nmake your body react in some strange ways.\n阳光和高温可能意味着野餐、公园和海滩之旅，但它们可能让你的身体以一些奇怪的方式作\n出反应。\n【信号】but 转折后才是真意。答案信号在 but they can make your body react in some strange\nways 的 but——前半 Sunshine and high temperatures can mean picnics and parks\nand trips to beaches 列举的野餐、公园、海滩都是 A/C/D 选项的来源（陷阱区），but\n之后才是文章真正要讲的主题：让身体出现奇怪反应。\n【替换】make your body react in some strange ways ↔ cause strange physical reactions\n同义改写：make…react ≈ cause…reactions\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 It creates a wonderful setting for dating. 借后文第二段提到 You may think that\nsummer is the perfect time to go on a date 里的 \"date\" 制造\"听到约会就想选\"的诱惑，\n但录音说的是\"你以为夏天适合约会\"，是一个被推翻的设定，不是\"阳光创造了约会良机\"这\n一主张。\n借用原文：You may think that summer is the perfect time to go on a date or chat with an attractive\nboy or girl.\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 It turns parks into picnic sites. 盗用了第一段铺垫中的 picnics and parks，但\npicnics 和 parks 在录音里只是 but 前的场景列举，根本没说\"阳光把公园变成野餐地\"——\n纯粹靠原词诱导的干扰。\n借用原文：Sunshine and high temperatures can mean picnics and parks and trips to beaches\nD) 未提及\n选项 It may result in a crowded beach. 盗用了 trips to beaches 里的 beaches 制造熟悉\n感，但录音里只有\"去海滩\"这件事，从未说 crowded（拥挤的）——\"拥挤的海滩\"是额外想\n象出来的细节。\n借用原文：trips to beaches", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What does the passage say is a negative side effect of water loss?", "options": ["A) Breathing difficulty.", "B) Bad breath.", "C) Excessive sweating.", "D) High blood pressure."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Bad breath.\n【定位】But excessive heat can cause you to lose water, which has been proven to have a pretty\nunattractive side effect—bad breath.\n但是过度的高温会让你失去水分，而水分流失已被证明有一个相当不雅观的副作用——\n口臭。\n【信号】破折号后的解释就是答案。答案信号是 a pretty unattractive side effect—bad breath 这\n个破折号结构：unattractive side effect 与题干 negative side effect 直接对应，破折号后\n面的 bad breath 就是这个 side effect 的具体所指。\n【替换】a pretty unattractive side effect ↔ a negative side effect of water loss\nunattractive 与 negative 同义\nbad breath ↔ Bad breath\n答案点原词复现\n【排除】A)\n选项 Breathing difficulty. 利用 breathing 与录音 bad breath 中 breath 的近形——只差\n一字，听感几乎相同，最容易让靠耳朵抓\"差不多的词\"的考生上当。但 breath 是\"口气\"，\nbreathing 是\"呼吸\"，录音从未提到呼吸困难。\n借用原文：bad breath\nC)\n选项 Excessive sweating. 利用了第三段 Sunshine can stop you from sweating, too. 里\n的 sweating 一词，但录音说的是 stop you from sweating（让你停止出汗），与\nexcessive sweating（过度出汗）方向恰好相反。\n借用原文：Sunshine can stop you from sweating, too.\nD) 未提及\n选项 High blood pressure. 听起来像是常见的高温副作用，但录音里讲水分流失的副作用\n时只提到 bad breath，从未出现 high blood pressure 或 blood pressure 之类的词——\n纯属凭常识脑补出来的干扰。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What is said to be a positive effect of sunshine?", "options": ["A) It protects people against bacteria.", "B) It enables people to build up endurance.", "C) It accelerates people's blood circulation.", "D) It provides people with extra energy."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It provides people with extra energy.\n【定位】However, the sun can have a positive influence on your body by raising your energy\nlevels. You might feel like the sun is giving you extra energy for your day, which is\nactually happening.\n然而，阳光可以通过提高你的能量水平对身体产生积极的影响。你可能感觉阳光在为你这一\n天提供额外的能量，而这其实是真的在发生。\n【信号】However 转折+ positive influence 极性词。答案信号是双重的：However 把话题从前\n文负面反应扳向正面作用；紧接着 positive influence 与题干 positive effect 直接对应；再\n后面给出具体方式 raising your energy levels 和加倍说明 giving you extra energy——\n三处叠加锁死答案句。\n【替换】raising your energy levels ↔ provides people with extra energy\nraise levels ≈ provide extra\nthe sun is giving you extra energy ↔ provides people with extra energy\ngive 与 provide 同义、extra energy 原词复现\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 It protects people against bacteria. 借用第三段 Bacteria accumulate because\nthere isn't enough water in your mouth to wash it away 里的 bacteria 制造熟悉感，但\n录音说的是\"细菌会累积\"，根本没说阳光保护人免受细菌侵害——把细菌的角色彻底搞反\n了。\n借用原文：Bacteria accumulate because there isn't enough water in your mouth to wash it away\nB) 未提及\n选项 It enables people to build up endurance. 中的 endurance（耐力）在录音里完全没\n有对应表述——既没说运动耐力，也没说体力持久；纯粹是凭\"阳光对身体好\"这种泛印象塞\n进来的诱选项。\nC)\n选项 It accelerates people's blood circulation. 的 blood circulation（血液循环）听起来\n非常\"科学\"，但录音里从未出现 blood、circulation 或任何对应表达；这是利用考生\"阳光\n促进血液循环\"的常识印象设计的陷阱。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the passage say about left-handed people?", "options": ["A) They are more likely to win in combat sports.", "B) They are in the minority among the population.", "C) They have a higher chance of joining sports teams.", "D) They have more disadvantages in getting ahead."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They are more likely to win in combat sports.\n【定位】Research found that they were overrepresented among fighters in combat sports. Left-handed\nfighters also had higher chances to win.\n研究发现，他们（左撇子）在搏击运动的拳手中占比偏高。左撇子拳手获胜的概率也更高。\n【信号】Research found 是研究结论信号。Research found that 是非常典型的\"研究结论\"信号，\n听力篇章一旦出现 Research found / Studies show / Scientists discovered，紧跟的那句\n几乎一定是出题点；这里跟着两句具体结论，正好对应一个选项。\n【替换】Left-handed fighters also had higher chances to win ↔\nThey are more likely to win in combat sports.\nhad higher chances to win = more likely to win\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB 项 They are in the minority among the population. 借了讲者 they were\noverrepresented among fighters in combat sports 里 overrepresented 涉及\"人群占\n比\"的话题，但讲者说的是\"在搏击拳手中占比偏高（overrepresented）\"，根本没说他们在\n总人口中是\"少数（in the minority）\"。一个是\"在某个子群里偏多\"，一个是\"在总人口里偏\n少\"，方向完全不一样。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 They have a higher chance of joining sports teams. 用 higher chance 这个原文里\n出现的搭配勾人，但 Left-handed fighters also had higher chances to win 说的是\"赢的\n概率更高\"，不是\"加入运动队的概率更高\"。原词 higher chances 被留下，宾语却从 win\n被偷换成了 joining sports teams。\nD) 与原文相反\nD 项 They have more disadvantages in getting ahead. 借了讲者开头 Left-handed\npeople can find it inconvenient to do certain things 里 inconvenient 的负面色彩，但\n讲者紧接着用 but 转折说 there are a number of advantages of being left-handed——\n全文真正落点是\"优势更多\"，不是\"不利更多\"。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What is said to be special about left-handed people?", "options": ["A) Their brain is more powerful than that of right-handed people.", "B) The left side of their brain is more powerful than its right side.", "C) They tend to be a lot more aggressive than right-handed people.", "D) Their brain has a stronger connection between its two sides."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Their brain has a stronger connection between its two sides.\n【定位】Left-handers may also be better at remembering events. The explanation is that the two brain\nspheres of left-handers are more strongly connected.\n左撇子在记忆事件方面可能也更出色。原因是左撇子的两个脑半球之间联系更紧密。\n【信号】The explanation is 是因果信号。The explanation is that 直接告诉考生\"接下来这句是解\n释/原因\"，是答案句典型路标；并且这句正好给出大脑结构特征 the two brain spheres of\nleft-handers are more strongly connected，与选项群里 brain 相关那一项直接对位。\n【替换】the two brain spheres of left-handers are more strongly connected ↔\nTheir brain has a stronger connection between its two sides.\nbrain spheres = two sides; strongly connected = stronger connection\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nA 项 Their brain is more powerful than that of right-handed people. 借了原文 brain\n这个核心词，但讲者只说\"两脑半球之间连接更强（more strongly connected）\"，并没有\n说\"整个大脑都比右撇子强\"。从\"连接性\"被扩大到了\"整体性能\"。\nB) 偷换概念\nB 项 The left side of their brain is more powerful than its right side. 借了原文 brain 与\nspheres 这两个原词的话题，却把讲者\"两个脑半球连接更强（two brain spheres … more\nstrongly connected）\"偷换成了\"左半球比右半球更强\"。一个讲\"连接\"，一个讲\"哪一侧更\n强\"，根本不在同一维度。\nC) 未提及\nC 项 They tend to be a lot more aggressive than right-handed people. 利用第 1 段\nfighters in combat sports 这种暗示攻击性的话题做联想，但讲者全篇没有任何\"左撇子更\n具攻击性（aggressive）\"的描述，更没有把它当作\"特殊之处\"提出。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "Why are left-handed people better at solving more difficult problems?", "options": ["A) They have a larger brain.", "B) It still remains unknown.", "C) It is related to their genes.", "D) They are better at reasoning."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It still remains unknown.\n【定位】An Italian study found that for simple arithmetic there was little difference between right- and\nleft-handed performance, but for more difficult problem-solving, left-handers won out. No\none understands the reason for this.\n一项意大利研究发现，在简单算术方面，左右撇子之间表现差别不大；但在更难的解题方\n面，左撇子胜出。然而没人弄清楚其中的原因。\n【信号】No one understands the reason 是\"未知\"信号。No one understands the reason for\nthis 是非常明确的\"原因尚不清楚\"型信号句，与选项 It still remains unknown. 的语义直接\n对应，是听力里最容易被忽视但出题率极高的句式之一。\n【替换】No one understands the reason for this ↔ It still remains unknown.\nNo one understands = remains unknown\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项 They have a larger brain. 想借第 2 段 brain 这个原词诱选，但讲者全篇没有任何\"左\n撇子大脑更大\"的描述。\nC) 未提及\nC 项 It is related to their genes. 在常识上很像\"科学解释\"，但讲者在第 3 段明明说 No\none understands the reason for this，根本没有把原因归到基因。\nD) 答非所问\nD 项 They are better at reasoning. 借了第 3 段讲到的\"在更难的解题里胜出（for more\ndifficult problem-solving, left-handers won out）\"的话题，但 problem-solving（解\n题）≠ reasoning（推理）；而且讲者从未把 reasoning 当作\"为什么更擅长难题\"的解释。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What should parents do about a left-handed child?", "options": ["A) Teach them how to perform tasks with their right hand.", "B) Help them fully develop their mathematical abilities.", "C) Encourage them to play fast-paced interactive sports.", "D) Advise them to choose jobs that require quick reactions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Encourage them to play fast-paced interactive sports.\n【定位】So if you have a left-handed child, you should encourage them to play table tennis, baseball\nand tennis.\n所以如果你的孩子是左撇子，就应该鼓励他们去打乒乓球、棒球和网球。\n【信号】you should encourage 是直给建议信号。you should encourage them 几乎是听力里最\n干脆的建议信号——should + encourage 直接对位题干 should do，紧跟的动作就是答案\n句的内容。\n【替换】encourage them to play table tennis, baseball and tennis ↔\nEncourage them to play fast-paced interactive sports.\n三项具体运动 = fast-paced interactive sports\nyou should encourage them ↔ Encourage them\nshould encourage 与 encourage 动宾完全对位\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA 项 Teach them how to perform tasks with their right hand. 让父母教孩子\"用右手做\n事\"，与全文为左撇子鸣不平、最后还鼓励他们发展左撇子优势的基调相反——讲者从未要\n求父母把孩子扳成右撇子。\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 项 Help them fully develop their mathematical abilities. 借了第 3 段 Being left-\nhanded might be a factor in mathematical ability 里 mathematical 这个原词，但讲者\n从未让父母\"帮孩子开发数学能力\"，那一段只是描述现象、没给建议。\nD) 偷换概念\nD 项 Advise them to choose jobs that require quick reactions. 借了原文 Those that\nrequire the fastest reaction 里 reaction 这个高识别度词做诱饵，但讲者说的是\"需要最快\n反应的运动（sports）\"，根本不是\"需要快速反应的职业（jobs）\"。修饰词 quick\nreactions 留下了，对象却被偷换。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) certificates", "C) connected", "D) demonstrates", "E) differences", "F) establish", "G) formats", "H) measured", "I) narrow", "J) permanent", "K) produces", "L) ranging", "M) relative", "N) submitting", "O) targeting"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) ranging\n动词\n【词性槽】follow-up periods 后接非谓语，需现在分词引出范围\n【依据】ranging from…to… 表\"随访期为 3 到 21 年\"，搭配天然成立\n代回即 follow-up periods __(26)__ from three to 21 years → 随访期\"范围从 3 年到 21\n年\"，ranging from A to B 是描述区间的固定说法，语义与语法都严丝合缝。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) certificates", "C) connected", "D) demonstrates", "E) differences", "F) establish", "G) formats", "H) measured", "I) narrow", "J) permanent", "K) produces", "L) ranging", "M) relative", "N) submitting", "O) targeting"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) measured\n动词\n【词性槽】Three of the papers + 空 + loneliness，主语后缺谓语，需动词（过去式）\n【依据】measured loneliness = 测量 / 衡量孤独，与并列句 looked at social isolation 对仗\n整句 Three of the papers __(27)__ loneliness, 18 looked at social isolation and two\nincluded both 在说\"各研究分别考察了什么\"：3 篇\"测量\"孤独、18 篇\"考察\"社交孤立、2\n篇两者都含。measured（过去式）既满足时态，又与 looked at、included 语义平行，自\n然贴切。\n【竞争词】A) acknowledge 形态 + 语义双不合：原形且\"承认孤独\"不通\nacknowledge 是原形（本空需过去式），且\"论文承认孤独\"不符\"研究考察某变量\"的语境，已被\n本空时态与语义双双排除（它真正归宿在第 34 空）。\nD) demonstrates 形态错：三单现在时，且\"论文证明孤独\"语义不通\ndemonstrates 是三单一般现在时，与本段过去时不符；\"3 篇论文证明孤独\"也讲不通（孤独不\n是被证明的命题），它真正属于第 33 空。\nF) establish 形态 + 语义不合：原形且\"确立孤独\"不通\nestablish 是原形，且\"确立 / 证实孤独\"不符此处\"考察了孤独这一变量\"的意思，归宿在第 35 空\nestablish cause and effect。\nK) produces 形态 + 语义不合：三单且\"产生孤独\"偏题\nproduces 是三单现在时（时态不符），\"论文产生孤独\"也不符\"研究测量某变量\"的语境，纯属\n同词性干扰。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 A) —— acknowledge 词性也对、又是研究类动词，极易误填 27。\n第 27 空 Three of the papers __(27)__ loneliness 有 5 个动词候选，acknowledge\"承\n认\"看着也像研究行为；但它是原形（本空需过去式）、且\"论文承认孤独\"不通。防坑：先用\n并列句 looked at 锁定过去式形态，再看\"考察 / 测量某变量\"的语义，measured 才对。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) certificates", "C) connected", "D) demonstrates", "E) differences", "F) establish", "G) formats", "H) measured", "I) narrow", "J) permanent", "K) produces", "L) ranging", "M) relative", "N) submitting", "O) targeting"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) certificates\n名词\n【词性槽】death + 空 作 for 的宾语，需名词\n【依据】death certificates = 死亡证明，与 medical records 并列、是判定死亡的依据\n上下文 the researchers depended on medical records and death __(28)__ for\ndetermining coronary events and strokes：研究者靠\"病历\"和\"死亡——\"来判定冠心病\n事件与中风。death certificates（死亡证明）正是与 medical records 并列的官方记录来\n源，搭配地道、语义贴切。\n【竞争词】E) differences 义不通：death differences 不成搭配\ndifferences 是\"差异\"，death differences 既非固定搭配也讲不通\"靠死亡差异来判定事件\"，与\nmedical records 并列在语义上对不上。\nG) formats 义不通：death formats（死亡格式）荒谬\nformats 指\"格式 / 版式\"，death formats 无此说法，也无法作为\"判定死亡事件的依据\"，纯属\n凑词性的干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) certificates", "C) connected", "D) demonstrates", "E) differences", "F) establish", "G) formats", "H) measured", "I) narrow", "J) permanent", "K) produces", "L) ranging", "M) relative", "N) submitting", "O) targeting"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) relative\n形容词\n【词性槽】the + 空 + risk，名词前作定语，需形容词\n【依据】relative risk = 相对风险，是医学统计术语，紧扣\"增加 29%\"\n下文 by 29 percent, and the risk of stroke by 32 percent 给的是百分比增幅，这正是流\n行病学里 relative risk（相对风险）的典型表述。relative 修饰 risk 构成专业术语，与本文\n医学研究语境高度契合。\n【竞争词】I) narrow 搭配 / 语义不当：narrow risk 不成立\nnarrow 是\"狭窄的\"，narrow risk 既非固定搭配，也无\"狭窄的风险\"这种讲法，与下文百分比增\n幅毫无关系。\nJ) permanent 语义偏：permanent risk 强调\"永久\"，与上下文\"增幅百分比\"不对应\npermanent\"永久的\"虽语法上能修饰 risk，但文中讨论的是风险\"增加了多少百分比\"，并未谈风\n险是否\"永久\"，语义指向跑偏，不如 relative 精准。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 J) —— permanent 修饰 risk 语法上通，易盖过术语词 relative。\n第 29 空 the __(29)__ risk … by 29 percent，permanent\"永久的风险\"语法挑不出错，考\n生若不识 relative risk 这一术语很容易误选；但下文给的是百分比增幅，对应的是 relative\nrisk（相对风险）。防坑：医学语境优先认\"专业术语搭配\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) certificates", "C) connected", "D) demonstrates", "E) differences", "F) establish", "G) formats", "H) measured", "I) narrow", "J) permanent", "K) produces", "L) ranging", "M) relative", "N) submitting", "O) targeting"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) differences\n名词\n【词性槽】There were no + 空，需复数名词\n【依据】no differences between men and women = 男女之间无差异\n结合上一句风险数据 increased the __(29)__ risk … by 29 percent, and the risk of stroke\nby 32 percent，紧跟一句 There were no __(30)__ between men and women 自然是\n说\"男女之间没有差异\"。difference between A and B 是高频固定搭配，语义与上下文完\n全咬合。\n【竞争词】G) formats 义不通：男女之间无\"格式\"差讲不通\nformats 指\"格式\"，\"男女之间没有格式\"毫无意义，也不与 between … and … 构成讨论\"两者异\n同\"的语义，只是同为名词的干扰项。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) certificates", "C) connected", "D) demonstrates", "E) differences", "F) establish", "G) formats", "H) measured", "I) narrow", "J) permanent", "K) produces", "L) ranging", "M) relative", "N) submitting", "O) targeting"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) targeting\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 on 后 + 空 + lonely people，需动名词（-ing）\n【依据】targeting lonely people = 针对孤独人群（采取政策），与后文呼应\n整句 People have tended to focus from a policy point of view on __(31)__ lonely\npeople to make them more __(32)__：政策一向\"针对\"孤独人群、好让他们更有联结。\ntarget（针对、以……为目标）接 lonely people 语义自然，且与后文 we should be\ntrying to prevent the risk factor in the first place 形成\"针对个体 vs 从源头预防\"的\n对照。\n【竞争词】N) submitting 语义不通：submitting lonely people 不成立\nsubmitting\"提交 / 使屈服\"接 lonely people 既无固定搭配也讲不通\"提交孤独的人\"，与\"政策\n针对某人群\"的语境完全不沾，纯属同为 -ing 的形态干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) certificates", "C) connected", "D) demonstrates", "E) differences", "F) establish", "G) formats", "H) measured", "I) narrow", "J) permanent", "K) produces", "L) ranging", "M) relative", "N) submitting", "O) targeting"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) connected\n形容词\n【词性槽】make them more + 空，make sb more + 形容词\n【依据】make them more connected = 让他们更有联结，正对\"孤独\"的反面\n上下文 focus … on __(31)__ lonely people to make them more __(32)__：针对孤独者，\n目的当然是让他们\"更有连接 / 更合群\"。connected（有联系的）正是 loneliness（孤独）\n的反面，与全文 social isolation or feelings of loneliness 的主题首尾呼应。\n【竞争词】I) narrow 语义反 / 不通：make people more narrow 不合逻辑\nnarrow\"狭窄的\"，\"让孤独者更狭窄\"既无逻辑也违背\"帮助孤独者\"的善意目的，方向完全反，只\n是凑形容词的干扰。\nJ) permanent 语义不通：make them more permanent 对象错位\npermanent\"永久的\"用来形容\"人\"不通——\"让人更永久\"没有意义，与\"缓解孤独\"的语境不搭，\n属同词性干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) certificates", "C) connected", "D) demonstrates", "E) differences", "F) establish", "G) formats", "H) measured", "I) narrow", "J) permanent", "K) produces", "L) ranging", "M) relative", "N) submitting", "O) targeting"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) demonstrates\n动词\n【词性槽】Our study + 空 + that 从句，主语后缺谓语、需三单动词\n【依据】study demonstrates that … = 研究表明……，引出研究结论\n下文是研究者的观点 if this is a risk factor, then we should be trying to prevent the risk\nfactor，前面用 demonstrates that（表明 / 说明）来引出这一论断最自然。study\ndemonstrates / shows that 是表达研究结论的高频搭配。\n【竞争词】K) produces 语义不通：study produces that 不成立\nproduces\"生产 / 产生\"是及物动词但不接 that 从句，\"研究产生（that 从句）\"语法 + 语义都不\n通，只是同为三单的形态干扰。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) certificates", "C) connected", "D) demonstrates", "E) differences", "F) establish", "G) formats", "H) measured", "I) narrow", "J) permanent", "K) produces", "L) ranging", "M) relative", "N) submitting", "O) targeting"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) acknowledge\n动词\n【词性槽】The authors + 空 + that 从句，复数主语后缺谓语，需动词原形\n【依据】authors acknowledge that … = 作者们承认 / 坦言（局限性）\nthat 从句的内容 this was a review of observational studies——研究者坦承本文只是\"观\n察性研究综述\"，这是在主动承认研究局限。acknowledge that（承认）正贴这种\"自陈不\n足\"的语气。\n【竞争词】F) establish 语义反：establish 是\"确立 / 证实\"，与\"坦承局限\"语气相反\nestablish\"确立 / 证实\"语法上也能接 that，但\"作者确立这是观察性研究综述\"不合自陈局限的语\n气；况且 establish 正是下一空 did not __(35)__ cause and effect 的答案，用过即划后这里更\n不会选它。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acknowledge", "B) certificates", "C) connected", "D) demonstrates", "E) differences", "F) establish", "G) formats", "H) measured", "I) narrow", "J) permanent", "K) produces", "L) ranging", "M) relative", "N) submitting", "O) targeting"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) establish\n动词\n【词性槽】did not + 空 + cause and effect，助动词 did 后需动词原形\n【依据】establish cause and effect = 确立 / 证实因果关系，是固定搭配\n结合 34 空整句 this was a review of observational studies and did not __(35)__ cause\nand effect——观察性研究\"无法证实因果关系\"，establish cause and effect 是讨论因果\n时的地道搭配，语义与\"观察性研究\"的局限完全咬合。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Students' financial aid remains unchanged even when tuition rises.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】学费涨而财援不变\n【定位】We did not know to ask—and the school did not readily point out—that even if tuition rises,\nmy financial aid package will stay the same.\n我们不懂得去问——学校也没有主动指出——即便学费上涨，我的财援方案也会保持不变。\n【改写】financial aid remains unchanged ↔ my financial aid package will stay the same\n同义替换：remains unchanged＝stay the same\neven when tuition rises ↔ even if tuition rises\n近乎原样：when＝if 让步\n【辨邻】 B B 段落点是 On paper, my expenses and my financial aid just about even out. Off\npaper, they don't.，谈的是当下平衡，与题干「学费上涨触发的不变」无关。\nOn paper, my expenses and my financial aid just about even out. Off paper, they don't.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Students may not be able to enjoy their state benefits when they go to college out of the state.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】出州后州福利享受不了\n【定位】It wasn't until after I had accepted admission to an out-of-state school that I learned that I\ncould not use my Ohio Medicaid on campus for anything other than emergency care. My\nbenefits became invalid the second I moved out of Ohio.\n直到接受了外州学校的录取之后，我才得知除急诊外我在校园里无法使用俄亥俄的\nMedicaid。我一搬出俄亥俄，我的福利就失效了。\n【改写】enjoy their state benefits ↔ use my Ohio Medicaid\n上位概括对下位实例\nmay not be able to enjoy ↔ My benefits became invalid\n同义改写：享受不了＝失效\nwhen they go to college out of the state ↔ the second I moved out of Ohio\n出州的同义表述", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The financial aid the author receives is supposed to cover all her college expenses.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】财援理应覆盖全部开支\n【定位】On paper, my expenses and my financial aid just about even out. Off paper, they don't.\n在纸面上，我的开支和财援大致持平；脱离纸面，则并非如此。\n【改写】supposed to cover all her college expenses ↔\nOn paper, my expenses and my financial aid just about even out\n理应覆盖＝纸面上正好抵平\nThe financial aid the author receives ↔ I receive over $57,000 yearly in financial aid\n作者所获财援的同指\n【辨邻】 I I 段焦点是 my financial aid package will stay the same 这类变动规则，没有「财援\n抵平全部开支」的陈述。\nColleges often offer the most generous packages during freshman year as a way to attract\nnew students.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "When the person who damages dorm facilities is not identified, students are required to share the cost.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】查不出肇事者就分摊损坏费\n【定位】The costs of any damage to the dorm, including elevators, bathrooms, and common areas, are\nbilled to every person on a dorm floor, or even the entire building if they do not know who\ncaused the damage.\n宿舍任何损坏（包括电梯、卫生间和公共区域）的费用，若查不出是谁造成的，就摊派给整\n层楼的每个人，甚至整栋楼。\n【改写】the person who damages dorm facilities is not identified ↔\nthey do not know who caused the damage\n同义改写：查不出肇事者\nstudents are required to share the cost ↔\nbilled to every person on a dorm floor, or even the entire building\n分摊＝按人头计费给整层", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Though it is difficult and embarrassing, students should make inquiries about what fees they have to pay and why.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】虽难堪也要追问该交哪些费\n【定位】If something does not look right, ask about it. If it still doesn't look right, negotiate it. … I\nunderstand the difficulty, and the embarrassment. But it is necessary.\n如果某项看着不对，就去问；如果问了还不对，就去交涉。……我理解其中的难处与难堪，\n但这是必要的。\n【改写】Though it is difficult and embarrassing ↔ I understand the difficulty, and the embarrassment\n形容词↔名词的同义转换\nmake inquiries about what fees they have to pay and why ↔ ask about it\n概括上位：make inquiries＝ask\n【辨邻】 K K 段落点是 Fill out a more comprehensive checklist, and be wary of listed prices\nthat seem too low，与题干的「ask／negotiate」无关。\nFill out a more comprehensive checklist, and be wary of listed prices that seem too low.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Today, many Americans have to go to college on student loans.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】靠助学贷款上大学\n【定位】They will also carry with them a huge student loan debt, in addition to countless \"hidden\"\nout-of-pocket costs paid for by their bank accounts and the bank accounts of their families.\n他们还会背上巨额的助学贷款债务，外加无数由本人及家庭账户支付的「隐藏」自付花费。\n【改写】go to college on student loans ↔ carry with them a huge student loan debt\n靠贷款上学＝背着助学贷款入学\nmany Americans ↔ thousands of college students from across the country\nmany＝thousands … across the country\n【辨邻】 B B 段 loans 出现在个人财援构成里 about $8,000 yearly in federally subsidized(有补\n贴的) and unsubsidized loans，范围是作者一人，与 many Americans 不符。\nabout $8,000 yearly in federally subsidized(有补贴的) and unsubsidized loans", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Receiving education in a private university in the nation's capital may change the author's future life and that of her family's.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】首都私立大学改变作者及家庭未来\n【定位】As a low-income student from a down-and-out Rust Belt community, these educational\nexperiences have enormous potential to brighten my future—and my family's future.\n作为一名来自衰败「铁锈地带」社区的低收入学生，这些教育经历极有可能照亮我的未来\n——以及我家庭的未来。\n【改写】a private university in the nation's capital ↔ an educational experience in Washington, D.C.\n首都的别称替换\nmay change the author's future life and that of her family's ↔\nbrighten my future—and my family's future\n改变未来＝照亮未来\n【辨邻】 B B 段 my well-respected, private, four-year university in Washington, D.C. 只描述\n学校与费用，缺少 may change the author's future life 这层。\nmy well-respected, private, four-year university in Washington, D.C., which boasts a yearly tuition\nof $44,046", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Students may no longer be qualified for financial aid if they perform poorly in school or if their family income has increased.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】成绩差或家庭收入升则失财援\n【定位】We were assured that, except for low grades or a raise in family income, no money would be\ntaken away.\n我们得到保证：除非成绩差或家庭收入提高，否则不会有钱被收回。\n【改写】perform poorly in school ↔ low grades\n同义改写：成绩差\ntheir family income has increased ↔ a raise in family income\n动词化与名词化的同义\nmay no longer be qualified for financial aid ↔ no money would be taken away\n失去资格＝钱被收回（正反同义）\n【辨邻】 B B 段 my financial aid package is a combination of federal grants and federal\nwork study 谈的是财援由什么组成，与扣援条件无关。\nmy financial aid package is a combination of federal grants and federal work study, university merit\nscholarships and financial aid awards", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "In addition to tuition, college students have to pay extra fees for the courses they take.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】学费之外还为所修课程付费\n【定位】Almost every class has an associated fee not included in the cost of tuition, most between $40\nand $100.\n几乎每门课都有一笔不含在学费里的附加费，大多在 40 到 100 美元之间。\n【改写】extra fees for the courses they take ↔ every class has an associated fee\nextra fee＝associated fee，course＝class\nIn addition to tuition ↔ not included in the cost of tuition\n学费之外＝不含在学费里\n【辨邻】 D D 段收费针对 any damage to the dorm，与题干「为所修课程付的费」不是一\n回事。\nThe costs of any damage to the dorm, including elevators, bathrooms, and common areas, are billed\nto every person on a dorm floor", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Some schools charge students a fee to their student accounts for using credit cards to pay bills.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】刷信用卡付账被收费\n【定位】My school charges an additional fee for the use of a credit card to settle outstanding charges,\nwhich can add upwards of 3% of the balance to your bill.\n我的学校对使用信用卡结清欠款收取额外费用，可能给账单加上高达余额 3% 的金额。\n【改写】charge students a fee … for using credit cards ↔\ncharges an additional fee for the use of a credit card\n信用卡收费的近乎原样复现\nto pay bills ↔ to settle outstanding charges\n付账单＝结清欠款", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What characterizes Japanese and Chinese foods?", "options": ["A) Variety.", "B) Flavor.", "C) Color.", "D) Naturalness."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What characterizes Japanese and Chinese foods? 是 what 直接询问客观特征，没\n有 infer/suggest/imply 之类的推断信号，要的就是原文写明的事实。\n【拆句】题干 What characterizes Japanese and Chinese foods? 是标准的 what 主语疑问句：\nwhat 作主语，characterizes 作谓语，Japanese and Chinese foods 是宾语，结构平铺直\n叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】He noted that Japanese people ate a wonderful variety of seaweeds, and that Chinese people\ntried to include at least five different colors in every meal.\n他注意到日本人吃种类繁多的海藻，中国人则努力让每餐都包含至少五种不同的颜色。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na wonderful variety of seaweeds ↔ Variety\nat least five different colors in every meal ↔ Variety\n预判核心是\"多样性\"，Variety 既能覆盖 variety of seaweeds，也能覆盖 five different\ncolors，是把两条并列事实的最大公约数提炼出来的上位词，与预判一致。\nB) 无中生有\n定位句 He noted that Japanese people ate a wonderful variety of seaweeds, and\nthat Chinese people tried to include at least five different colors in every meal. 通\n篇讲海藻种类和颜色，没有任何 flavor/taste 的描述；选项 Flavor 在原文里没有对应。\nC) 以偏概全\n选项 Color 只对应 at least five different colors in every meal 这一半，根本不能解释\na wonderful variety of seaweeds——日本人吃海藻种类多与颜色无关；以局部冒充\n整体。\nD) 无中生有\nNaturalness 意为\"天然性、未加工\"，原文定位句没有 natural/unprocessed/raw 之类\n的词；海藻和颜色多样不等于\"自然\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What is typical of Americans in the way of eating?", "options": ["A) They emphasize nutrition.", "B) They tend to eat quite a lot.", "C) They prioritize convenience.", "D) They care about flavors."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is typical of Americans in the way of eating? 用 typical 直接问\"典型\"，要求\n原文里被作者明确点出的美国人吃饭特征。\n【拆句】题干主干是 what is typical of Americans，in the way of eating 是修饰 typical 的范围介\n词短语；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】For starters, Americans eat much bigger portions than people in other countries.\n首先，美国人吃的分量比其他国家的人要大得多。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文 We don't prioritize eating seasonally or locally, and we also add lots of salt,\nsugar and thickening agents to our foods 说的是美国人\"不\"重视当季当地、还要加\n大量盐糖增稠剂——明显是不健康、不重营养的做法，emphasize nutrition 与原文方\n向相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\neat much bigger portions ↔ tend to eat quite a lot\nthan people in other countries ↔ tend to\n预判核心是\"美国人吃得多\"，They tend to eat quite a lot. 把定位句的数量比较结论直\n接保留下来，没做引申，与预判完全一致。\nC) 无中生有\n原文讲的是分量大、不重视当季当地、爱加盐糖——没出现\nconvenience/quick/fast/easy 等\"便利\"语义的词；选项 prioritize convenience 在原\n文中无依据。\nD) 无中生有\n原文谈份量、当季当地、盐糖增稠剂，未涉及 flavor/taste；care about flavors 在原文\n中没有对应表述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What features are in Mediterranean, Nordic and Okinawan diets?", "options": ["A) Fruits and vegetables.", "B) Olive oil and red wine.", "C) Seasonal local produce.", "D) Unprocessed ingredients."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What features are in Mediterranean, Nordic and Okinawan diets? 直接询问几种\n饮食\"具备什么特征\"，答案应是原文明写的共同特征。\n【拆句】题干 What features are in Mediterranean, Nordic and Okinawan diets?：what\nfeatures 作主语、are 系动词、in Mediterranean, Nordic and Okinawan diets 是表语介\n词短语，把范围限定在这三种饮食里；结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】Contrast that to the healthy Mediterranean, Nordic and Okinawan diets listed in Pasternak's\nbook. They all seem to stick to the ethos(特质) of regional, seasonal produce.\n把这一点（指美国人的饮食习惯）与 Pasternak 书中列出的健康的地中海、北欧和冲绳饮食\n对比一下。它们似乎都坚守着\"当地、当季食材\"这一特质。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nfruits, vegetables 出现在 P4 段对地中海饮食的展开里；但题目问的是三种饮食的共有\n特征，Fruits and vegetables 只是地中海饮食的成分，不能说北欧和冲绳饮食也以这两\n样为主——选项错把局部例证当全局共性。\nB) 以偏概全\nolive oil 与 red wine 同样出现在 P4 段对地中海饮食的描写里，是地中海饮食的特色，\n但不是北欧和冲绳饮食的特征；选项把地中海饮食的标志当成三地共同点。\nC) ✓ 正确\nregional, seasonal produce ↔ Seasonal local produce\n预判核心\"当地、当季、食材\"三层在 Seasonal local produce 中一一对应，无遗漏；这\n是细节题正确项最干净的样子——预判几个要素，选项几乎逐词对接。\nD) 无中生有\n原文中谈到三地饮食的共同点只有 regional, seasonal produce（当地当季食材），并\n未出现 unprocessed/raw/whole 之类强调\"未加工\"的语义；Unprocessed\ningredients 是凭健康饮食的常识印象生造的特征。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What do people in most healthy eating cultures have in common?", "options": ["A) They get the whole family to eat at a table.", "B) They eat their meals regularly and punctually.", "C) They consume plenty of cereal for breakfast.", "D) They attach great importance to their meals."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do people in most healthy eating cultures have in common? 直问\"共同之\n处\"，不含推断信号词，只要找原文的归纳句即可。\n【拆句】题干 What do people in most healthy eating cultures have in common?：what 作\nhave 的宾语前置，主语是 people in most healthy eating cultures；have…in common\n是固定搭配\"有共同点\"，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Most other healthy eating cultures also make meals an event—say, multiple courses around\nthe family table, or a glass or two of red wine at a long lunch—as opposed to hastily wolfing\ndown handfuls of cereal above the kitchen sink and calling it dinner.\n大多数其他健康饮食文化都把吃饭当作一件\"大事\"——例如全家围桌吃几道菜，或者午餐时\n悠悠喝上一两杯红酒——而不是匆匆抓把麦片站在水槽边解决一顿。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nget the whole family to eat at a table 只对应 multiple courses around the family\ntable 这一个例子，没覆盖另一例 a glass or two of red wine at a long lunch，更没覆\n盖\"重视用餐\"这个主旨；以一个例子冒充整段共性。\nB) 无中生有\n原文 P5 只说 long lunch（漫长的午餐），完全没有 regularly/punctually/on time\n这类\"规律/守时\"的语义；选项 eat their meals regularly and punctually 在原文里无\n对应。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文中 handfuls of cereal above the kitchen sink 是用来作\"反例\"的——讽刺美国人\n草率吃法的——而选项 consume plenty of cereal for breakfast 反而把这条反例当成\n健康文化的优点，方向南辕北辙。\nD) ✓ 正确\nmake meals an event ↔ attach great importance to their meals\n预判核心\"重视用餐\"与 attach great importance to their meals 字字对应；同时这\n一表达覆盖了原文后面三个例证（围桌、几道菜、长午餐），是所有例证背后的统一\n主旨。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "Compared with the average American, people in healthy societies ___________.", "options": ["A) Walk at least 4 miles a day", "B) Do considerably more walking", "C) Pay more attention to body shape", "D) Consume a lot more organic food"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Compared with the average American, people in healthy societies ___________.\n是补全句、内容来自原文的事实性对比陈述，不要求做推断，按细节题走。\n【拆句】题干 Compared with the average American, people in healthy societies ___________.\n是不完整陈述句：Compared with the average American 是过去分词短语作状语，逻\n辑主语是后面的 people in healthy societies；选项是句子谓语的延续，需保证主谓搭配\n自然。\n【定位】The only overlapping feature in most of these healthy countries is that they all walk way more\nthan the average American.\n这些健康国家唯一的共同特征是：他们走路都远远多于普通美国人。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 if someone's walking four miles more than you each day 中的 four miles 是\nPasternak 的进一步举例性数字，且是\"多走 4 英里\"（每天比对方多 4 英里），不是\"至\n少走 4 英里\"；选项 walk at least 4 miles a day 把\"多走的差值\"偷换成\"日行的总数\"，\n并加了原文没有的 at least。\nB) ✓ 正确\nwalk way more than the average American ↔ do considerably more walking\n预判核心\"走路远多于美国人\"被 do considerably more walking 完整保留——比较级\nmore、程度副词 considerably（= 原文 way）、活动 walking，三层信息一一对应。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 they're going to be a lot thinner 是\"会瘦很多\"——这是走路多带来的结果，不\n是健康社会的人\"在意身材\"这一主观倾向；选项 pay more attention to body shape\n是把客观结果错读成主观态度。\nD) 无中生有\n整篇文章谈份量、海藻、五色、当地当季食材、用餐重视度、走路——通篇没出现\norganic（有机的）这个词；选项 consume a lot more organic food 在原文中完全无\n对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is the finding of the new study?", "options": ["A) Emotional intelligence helps people get along with others.", "B) Emotional intelligence is generally beneficial to its bearer.", "C) People who are aware of others' feelings may experience more stress.", "D) People who are emotionally stressed may have trouble making friends."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the finding of the new study?直接问研究的发现，没有 infer/imply/suggest 等\n推断信号词，答案应能在原文中找到客观对应。\n【拆句】Q51是一个简单 What is + 主语 + of 短语的疑问句，主干是the finding of the new\nstudy，of 引导的介宾短语限定 finding 的归属，无从句、无否定，结构平铺直叙，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】But a new study suggests that being sensitive to people's feelings may sometimes come with\nan extra dose of stress.\n但一项新研究指出，对他人情绪敏感，有时会带来额外的压力。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nEmotional intelligence helps people get along with others.说的是「情商有助于与\n人相处」，这是Recognizing when a friend or colleague feels sad, angry or\nsurprised is key to getting along with others.这句旧常识，而不是题干问的the new\nstudy的发现。\nB) 与原文相反\nEmotional intelligence is generally beneficial to its bearer.恰恰是新研究要\nchallenge的主流观点，原文用This and other research challenge the prevailing\nview that emotional intelligence is uniformly beneficial to its bearer.明确反对它，\n把它当 finding 是把靶子当结论。\nC) ✓ 正确\nbeing sensitive to people's feelings ↔ People who are aware of others' feelings\ncome with an extra dose of stress ↔ may experience more stress\n预判的「敏锐感知他人情绪的人会承受更多压力」与People who are aware of others'\nfeelings may experience more stress.从主语到谓语完全对应，且程度词 sometimes\n与 may 等值，无超译。\nD) 无中生有\nPeople who are emotionally stressed may have trouble making friends.说的是\n「情绪压力大→交不到朋友」——原文谈的方向恰好相反：「敏锐感知情绪→压力更\n大」，且原文根本没提「交不到朋友」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What was the purpose of psychologist Myriam Bechtoldt's experiment?", "options": ["A) To define different types of human feelings.", "B) To assess the impacts of being emotionally clever.", "C) To demonstrate how to distinguish different feelings.", "D) To identify gender differences in emotional intelligence."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What was the purpose of psychologist Myriam Bechtoldt's experiment?问的是实验目\n的，是可在原文中找到客观依据的事实信息，不是推断或主旨。\n【拆句】Q52主干是What was the purpose ... experiment?，of 后面跟一长串名词同位修饰\npsychologist Myriam Bechtoldt's experiment，结构是简单的 What + be + 主语，没有\n从句和否定，无陷阱。\n【定位】In a study, psychologist Myriam Bechtoldt of the Frankfurt School of Finance and\nManagement in Germany asked 166 male university students a series of questions to measure\ntheir emotional intelligence.\n在一项研究中，德国法兰克福金融与管理学院的心理学家 Myriam Bechtoldt 向 166 名男\n大学生提了一系列问题，以测量他们的情商。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nTo define different types of human feelings.意为「定义不同种类的人类情绪」，但\n原文they showed the students photographs of people's faces and asked them to\nwhat extent feelings such as happiness or disgust were being expressed.中「看脸\n辨情绪」是测量情商的手段细节，不是研究目的。\nB) ✓ 正确\nto measure their emotional intelligence ↔ To assess the impacts of being emotionally clever\nThe findings suggest that some people may be too emotionally clever for their own good ↔\nthe impacts of being emotionally clever\n定位句给出的是研究手段（测量情商加测压力激素），而P1与P3共同指明，这一手段服\n务于「评估情商高带来的影响」这一目的，与B完全对应。\nC) 无中生有\nTo demonstrate how to distinguish different feelings.意为「演示如何区分不同情\n绪」，但实验是测量学生情商，不是教学生区分情绪；原文没有 demonstrate / teach\n类动词。\nD) 无中生有\nTo identify gender differences in emotional intelligence.说研究目的是识别性别差\n异，但实验对象是166 male university students——全是男生，根本没设性别对照；\n末段还提到More research is needed to see how exactly the relation between\nemotional intelligence and stress would play out in women——女性差异是「待研\n究」，不是本研究的目的。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the finding of Myriam Bechtoldt's study indicate?", "options": ["A) Greater emotional cleverness means less trouble in one's life.", "B) Emotional intelligence helps people succeed in job interviews.", "C) People's psychological wellbeing is related to various factors.", "D) People may suffer from having a high emotional intelligence"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the finding of Myriam Bechtoldt's study indicate?问的是研究结果所「表\n明」的内容，原文有研究者亲口下的结论句作为依据，仍属细节题，不需要超出原文做\n推断。\n【拆句】Q53结构是 What does + 主语 + indicate？主语the finding of Myriam Bechtoldt's\nstudy由 of 介宾限定，全句是简单宾语从句式提问，无否定无比较，无陷阱。\n【定位】The findings suggest that some people may be too emotionally clever for their own good,\nsays Bechtoldt.\n研究者 Bechtoldt 说，研究结果表明，有些人的情商可能高过头反害了自己。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nGreater emotional cleverness means less trouble in one's life.说情商越高麻烦越\n少，与原文Sometimes you can be so good at something that it causes trouble完\n全相反。\nB) 无中生有\nEmotional intelligence helps people succeed in job interviews.说情商帮人在求职面\n试中成功——但原文里give job talks in front of judges只是实验任务场景（求职演\n讲），原文从未说情商高能让人面试成功；恰恰相反，情商高的人压力更大。\nC) 答非所问\nPeople's psychological wellbeing is related to various factors.「心理健康与多种因\n素有关」在常识上无可指摘，但原文研究只讲情商与压力的关系，没有讨论「多种因\n素」，是模糊化的万能选项。\nD) ✓ 正确\nsome people may be too emotionally clever for their own good ↔\nPeople may suffer from having a high emotional intelligence\nSometimes you can be so good at something that it causes trouble ↔\nsuffer from having a high emotional intelligence\n预判「情商高反受其害」与People may suffer from having a high emotional\nintelligence在主语、谓语、宾语三层完全咬合，且都保留 may 的不确定语气，是定位\n句的标准同义改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What do we learn about emotional intelligence from a number of studies?", "options": ["A) People suffering from depression are emotionally immature.", "B) People who look at the dark side of life often feel depressed.", "C) Some people may take advantage of it and benefit themselves.", "D) Some people may lack it and are easily manipulated by others."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about emotional intelligence from a number of studies?问的是\n「从多项研究我们得知什么」，对应原文综述段的事实罗列，不是推断或主旨。\n【拆句】Q54主干是 What do we learn? 后接 about + 话题 + from + 来源两个介宾短语限定\nlearn 的范围；about 限定话题、from 限定信息来源，两个限定都不能丢，结构清晰、无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Furthermore, several studies have implied that emotional intelligence can be used to\nmanipulate others for personal gains.\n此外，多项研究还暗示，情商可被用来操纵他人以谋取私利。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nPeople suffering from depression are emotionally immature.说抑郁者情绪不成熟\n——但原文emotionally perceptive people might be particularly influenced by\nfeelings of depression and hopelessness说的是「情绪敏锐者更易受抑郁影响」，主\n语和因果都被改了。\nB) 偷换概念\nPeople who look at the dark side of life often feel depressed.说「看人生消极面的\n人常抑郁」——这是日常常识，但原文谈的是「情绪敏锐者易受抑郁情绪影响」，主语\n错配、因果也错。\nC) ✓ 正确\nemotional intelligence can be used to manipulate others for personal gains ↔\nSome people may take advantage of it and benefit themselves\n预判的「情商可被用来操纵他人谋私」与Some people may take advantage of it and\nbenefit themselves在主语、动词短语、目的三段完全对位，且 may 与 can 都是表\n「可能性」的弱情态，无超译。\nD) 与原文相反\nSome people may lack it and are easily manipulated by others.说「有些人缺乏情\n商，因此易被他人操纵」——但原文谈的是「情商高的人可以操纵别人」，而不是「缺\n情商的人被人操纵」，施事与受事正好反过来。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author suggest sensitive individuals do?", "options": ["A) Avoid burdening themselves with others' feelings.", "B) Rid themselves of worries over worldly affairs.", "C) Learn to cope with people's negative feelings.", "D) Help people to deal with their troubles in life."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest sensitive individuals do?问作者给敏感者的具体建议，\n原文应有明示的建议句作为依据。\n【拆句】Q55是 What does + 主语 + suggest + 宾语从句的疑问句；宾语从句省略 that，形式为\nsensitive individuals do——主语 sensitive individuals + 动词原形 do，是 suggest sb.\n(should) do 的虚拟语气省略式，do 表「做（什么）」，对应选项里的动词短语。\n【定位】For example, some sensitive individuals may assume responsibility for other people's sadness\nor anger, which ultimately stresses them out. Remember, as Bechtoldt says, \"you are not\nresponsible for how other people feel.\"\n举例来说，一些敏感的人可能会替别人的悲伤或愤怒揽责任，结果反而把自己压垮。记住\nBechtoldt 的话：你不必为别人的感受负责。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nyou are not responsible for how other people feel ↔\nAvoid burdening themselves with others' feelings\nsome sensitive individuals may assume responsibility for other people's sadness or anger, which\nultimately stresses them out\n↔ burdening themselves with others' feelings\n预判的「别替别人的情绪扛锅」与Avoid burdening themselves with others' feelings\n在动词、宾语、隐含主语三层完全对位，且都保留「避免、不要」的建议口吻。\nB) 无中生有\nRid themselves of worries over worldly affairs.说「摆脱对世俗琐事的忧虑」——\n但原文谈的是「别人的情绪」，不是「世俗琐事」；worldly affairs 在原文完全没有\n对应。\nC) 偷换概念\nLearn to cope with people's negative feelings.建议敏感者去「应对他人的负面情\n绪」——但原文you are not responsible for how other people feel给的建议恰恰相\n反：「不必为别人感受负责」，根本不主张主动去处理别人的情绪。要注意原文确实有\nlearn to also properly cope with emotions—both others' and your own，但那是\n给所有人的总建议、且强调「包括自己的」；针对 sensitive individuals 的针对性建议\n是 not responsible 这一句。\nD) 与原文相反\nHelp people to deal with their troubles in life.建议敏感者去「帮别人解决生活中的麻\n烦」——这与作者的建议方向完全相反，作者明示you are not responsible for how\nother people feel。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
